WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.461 align:middle line:84%
[SQUEAKING]
[RUSTLING] [CLICKING]

00:00:06.461 --> 00:00:17.210 align:middle line:90%


00:00:17.210 --> 00:00:20.390 align:middle line:84%
GLENN ELLISON: So anyway,
today's lecture, I was doing--

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:22.470 align:middle line:84%
I did static
competition last week.

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:24.650 align:middle line:84%
Today, my plan is I'm going
to start with empirics

00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:26.278 align:middle line:90%
of static competition.

00:00:26.278 --> 00:00:28.820 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to fill in, in the
middle, some extra theory that I

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:32.023 align:middle line:84%
didn't have time to
do last Wednesday.

00:00:32.023 --> 00:00:33.440 align:middle line:84%
And then the paper
at the end, I'm

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:35.690 align:middle line:84%
going to do very, very quickly
because I just was reading it

00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:37.398 align:middle line:84%
over, making up the
notes, and I realized

00:00:37.398 --> 00:00:41.480 align:middle line:84%
that Tobias is going to be
spending an awful lot of time

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:44.570 align:middle line:84%
next Monday explaining
what the model is

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:45.900 align:middle line:90%
about and the estimation of it.

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:47.540 align:middle line:84%
And I figured that
it'd be much better

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:48.990 align:middle line:84%
to wait until after
he's done that

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:51.290 align:middle line:84%
and you can just look
at my slides on it.

00:00:51.290 --> 00:00:53.700 align:middle line:84%
And I'll just stick
with basic comments.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:56.210 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, I'm
going to first cover

00:00:56.210 --> 00:00:58.170 align:middle line:84%
one of my favorite
papers in the course.

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:01.670 align:middle line:84%
So this is Bresnahan's paper,
"Competition and Collusion

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:06.260 align:middle line:84%
in the American Automobile
Industry: The 1955 Price War."

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:08.960 align:middle line:84%
I should say, in some sense,
it's one of my favorite papers.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:11.210 align:middle line:84%
I took the star off it this
year because in some ways,

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:13.610 align:middle line:84%
I think as the paper
gets more and more dated,

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:16.200 align:middle line:84%
you can appreciate it more by
hearing about what it does than

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:17.400 align:middle line:90%
by actually reading it.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:19.860 align:middle line:84%
There just many things that
you read and you're like, wow,

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:22.240 align:middle line:84%
that sounds like a 40-year-old
way to do something.

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:25.440 align:middle line:84%
And so I'll just leave out the
40-year-old parts that were

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:28.350 align:middle line:84%
a bit awkward and probably
had some trouble publishing it

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:30.910 align:middle line:84%
to begin with, and I'll
give you the main ideas.

00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:37.650 align:middle line:84%
So this is a paper on the IO
- Economic History boundary.

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:44.350 align:middle line:84%
The applied topic is what caused
the 1955 surge in US auto sales,

00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:46.380 align:middle line:84%
and it has this
interesting hypothesis,

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:49.920 align:middle line:84%
which is that the 1955
surge in auto sales

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:53.430 align:middle line:84%
was due to a breakdown in
collusion that, at that time,

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:56.110 align:middle line:84%
there were very few foreign
cars sold in the United States.

00:01:56.110 --> 00:01:58.240 align:middle line:84%
There were largely three
American automakers.

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:01.350 align:middle line:84%
And his hypothesis is that
the three US automakers were

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:04.330 align:middle line:84%
colluding to set
monopoly prices,

00:02:04.330 --> 00:02:07.230 align:middle line:84%
and then in 1955, the
collusion broke down,

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:09.600 align:middle line:84%
prices dropped, many
more cars were sold.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:12.550 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, and then the firms
got it figured out again.

00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:16.410 align:middle line:90%
In 1956, prices went back up.

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:20.300 align:middle line:84%
The paper, besides having an
interesting historical fact,

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:24.080 align:middle line:84%
it has been really influential
as a model for future work.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:28.400 align:middle line:84%
In some sense, I see this as the
first structural empirical paper

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:30.710 align:middle line:84%
in IO, and I think that
what you can think of

00:02:30.710 --> 00:02:34.880 align:middle line:84%
is that really many future
papers have picked up

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:37.032 align:middle line:84%
on Bresnahan's methodology
and adopted this

00:02:37.032 --> 00:02:38.490 align:middle line:84%
as a different way
of doing things.

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:41.720 align:middle line:84%
So I will explain what
it was that Bresnahan

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:43.610 align:middle line:90%
was trying to do in this paper.

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:47.690 align:middle line:84%
But first on the applied
topic, you know, here's--

00:02:47.690 --> 00:02:49.988 align:middle line:84%
he starts with a simple
table of car sales

00:02:49.988 --> 00:02:51.530 align:middle line:84%
by year in the United
States, and you

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:56.210 align:middle line:84%
get this striking
finding, which is in 1954,

00:02:56.210 --> 00:02:58.770 align:middle line:84%
US automakers produce
5.5 million cars.

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:02.040 align:middle line:84%
In 1955, they produce
7.9 million cars.

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:06.500 align:middle line:84%
In 1956, they produced
5.8 million cars.

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:08.010 align:middle line:84%
Cars are a big part
of the economy,

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:11.120 align:middle line:84%
and the cars being produced
going up by 40% in one year

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:13.160 align:middle line:90%
is a really striking fact.

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:17.340 align:middle line:84%
If you look at it, sales in
dollars, it's still a big jump.

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:20.580 align:middle line:84%
The jump in dollars
of car sales is not

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:23.370 align:middle line:84%
as big as the jump
in unit car sales.

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:25.530 align:middle line:84%
In part, that's because
prices were lower,

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:30.270 align:middle line:84%
and in part, that's because
people did buy smaller cars.

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:33.450 align:middle line:84%
And then if you look at what
was going on in the US economy,

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:36.070 align:middle line:84%
it was a boom time
in the US economy.

00:03:36.070 --> 00:03:37.710 align:middle line:84%
You can see non-auto
durable goods

00:03:37.710 --> 00:03:40.680 align:middle line:84%
spending went from
14.5 to 16 billion

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:44.290 align:middle line:84%
and then continued increasing
to 17 billion the next year.

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:46.560 align:middle line:84%
So it was a good year
for the US economy,

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:50.280 align:middle line:84%
but not a 40% larger
increase in car sales

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:51.420 align:middle line:90%
boom in the US economy.

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:59.190 align:middle line:84%
There really are not 40% boom in
car sales general macro shocks.

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:01.890 align:middle line:84%
You can find explanations
in the popular press

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:06.660 align:middle line:84%
of what caused the 1955 thing
as a cultural phenomenon.

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:08.790 align:middle line:84%
Here's a quote from
Car Talk describing

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:13.110 align:middle line:84%
this, where they talk about
how rock music and drive-in

00:04:13.110 --> 00:04:15.070 align:middle line:84%
restaurants were
becoming a thing

00:04:15.070 --> 00:04:20.180 align:middle line:84%
and there were sleek new
designs for cars and whatever.

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:23.990 align:middle line:84%
Apparently back in the 1950s,
Paul Samuelson taught both

00:04:23.990 --> 00:04:25.895 align:middle line:90%
[14.]121--

00:04:25.895 --> 00:04:27.830 align:middle line:84%
taught both in the
micro sequence,

00:04:27.830 --> 00:04:31.340 align:middle line:84%
[14.]121 through [14.]124, and
in the econometric sequence,

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:32.700 align:middle line:90%
[14.]381, [14.]382.

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:34.880 align:middle line:84%
I think the numbers were
still the same 50 years

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:38.180 align:middle line:90%
or 70 years ago, that is now.

00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:42.860 align:middle line:84%
But Paul, apparently--
this was pre-14.192,

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:46.070 align:middle line:84%
but there was a required
second year econometrics paper.

00:04:46.070 --> 00:04:48.770 align:middle line:84%
And Paul Samuelson apparently
was teaching the second year

00:04:48.770 --> 00:04:51.590 align:middle line:84%
econometrics course, would say
that he would flunk any paper

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:55.280 align:middle line:84%
that claimed it was providing
an explanation for 1955 sales,

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:58.118 align:middle line:84%
where by "explanation" he
meant you're regressing sales

00:04:58.118 --> 00:05:00.410 align:middle line:84%
and a bunch of right-hand
side variables like the price

00:05:00.410 --> 00:05:03.770 align:middle line:90%
of steel and whatever.

00:05:03.770 --> 00:05:06.000 align:middle line:90%
OK, so what were cars in 1955?

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:08.100 align:middle line:84%
So I've got some
illustrations here.

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:13.280 align:middle line:84%
So 1955 Mercury Montclair,
1955 Chevy Bel Air,

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:15.520 align:middle line:90%
1955 Ford Thunderbird.

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:19.560 align:middle line:84%
You can see the '55
Thunderbird is a very cute car.

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:22.900 align:middle line:84%
The other cars just look
pretty generic to me.

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:27.450 align:middle line:84%
For contrast, here's the 1955
Chevy Bel Air versus the 1954

00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:29.880 align:middle line:84%
Chevy Bel Air, and
you can see that they

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:32.490 align:middle line:84%
seem to have taken away a
little bit of this wheel thing

00:05:32.490 --> 00:05:36.000 align:middle line:84%
that's stuck out here and
they smoothed out the grille.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:38.620 align:middle line:84%
Instead of these big things,
they went to small things.

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:41.280 align:middle line:84%
You know, it fits with
the sleeker car theory,

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:45.073 align:middle line:84%
but it doesn't look all
that different to me.

00:05:45.073 --> 00:05:46.740 align:middle line:84%
Perhaps someday--
perhaps today, someone

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:48.330 align:middle line:84%
could write a machine
learning thing

00:05:48.330 --> 00:05:50.340 align:middle line:84%
judging the sleekness
of cars and seeing

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:52.802 align:middle line:84%
how much the sleekness of
cars affects increased sales

00:05:52.802 --> 00:05:53.760 align:middle line:90%
or something like that.

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:54.302 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:05:54.302 --> 00:05:56.910 align:middle line:90%


00:05:56.910 --> 00:05:59.280 align:middle line:84%
OK, anyway, so his
hypothesis is that this

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:02.165 align:middle line:84%
was caused by a breakdown in
collusion among the big three.

00:06:02.165 --> 00:06:05.040 align:middle line:90%


00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:08.183 align:middle line:84%
In general, if
you're writing a--

00:06:08.183 --> 00:06:10.600 align:middle line:84%
there are several types of
empirical papers one can write.

00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:12.310 align:middle line:84%
If you're writing
a, here's a fact.

00:06:12.310 --> 00:06:14.940 align:middle line:84%
I have an explanation
for that fact.

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:17.770 align:middle line:84%
Generally, if the
fact is well-known

00:06:17.770 --> 00:06:19.900 align:middle line:84%
and you're proposing
an explanation for it,

00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:21.550 align:middle line:84%
there are a thousand
explanations

00:06:21.550 --> 00:06:23.120 align:middle line:84%
for any fact you
can come up with.

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:24.662 align:middle line:84%
So generally, the
way you try to make

00:06:24.662 --> 00:06:27.730 align:middle line:84%
the case that your explanation
is correct and convincing

00:06:27.730 --> 00:06:30.790 align:middle line:84%
is to think of a lot
of other predictions

00:06:30.790 --> 00:06:33.010 align:middle line:84%
that go along with
your explanation

00:06:33.010 --> 00:06:34.678 align:middle line:84%
that people haven't
talked about yet,

00:06:34.678 --> 00:06:36.970 align:middle line:84%
and then show that here's a
bunch of other implications

00:06:36.970 --> 00:06:37.512 align:middle line:90%
of my theory.

00:06:37.512 --> 00:06:39.512 align:middle line:84%
Now I'm going to look at
the data in more detail

00:06:39.512 --> 00:06:41.500 align:middle line:84%
and show these other
implications of my theory

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:43.660 align:middle line:90%
are true as well.

00:06:43.660 --> 00:06:47.290 align:middle line:84%
And that's what Bresnahan
is trying to do.

00:06:47.290 --> 00:06:52.210 align:middle line:84%
And his idea is that motivated
by then recent literature

00:06:52.210 --> 00:06:53.830 align:middle line:84%
on differentiated
product competition

00:06:53.830 --> 00:06:57.880 align:middle line:84%
was that if there was a shift
from collusion to competition,

00:06:57.880 --> 00:07:01.210 align:middle line:84%
it ought to have a different
effect on different car models.

00:07:01.210 --> 00:07:04.900 align:middle line:84%
In particular, car models that
were close in product space

00:07:04.900 --> 00:07:09.700 align:middle line:84%
to other car models would see
a much bigger drop in price

00:07:09.700 --> 00:07:11.810 align:middle line:84%
than car models that
were very distinctive.

00:07:11.810 --> 00:07:16.460 align:middle line:84%
So on my previous slide, you
can see the Ford Thunderbird.

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:18.740 align:middle line:90%
It was an original car.

00:07:18.740 --> 00:07:21.260 align:middle line:84%
It looks very different
from those other cars.

00:07:21.260 --> 00:07:23.290 align:middle line:84%
Maybe if you're colluding
or in competition,

00:07:23.290 --> 00:07:25.415 align:middle line:84%
it doesn't matter so much
for the price of the Ford

00:07:25.415 --> 00:07:26.000 align:middle line:90%
Thunderbird.

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:28.030 align:middle line:84%
You still have a
lot of market power.

00:07:28.030 --> 00:07:29.650 align:middle line:84%
But at least to
my mind, if you're

00:07:29.650 --> 00:07:34.000 align:middle line:84%
looking at the Mercury Montclair
versus the Chevy Bel Air,

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:36.070 align:middle line:84%
I would have to spend
some time learning

00:07:36.070 --> 00:07:38.240 align:middle line:84%
to recognize the difference
between those two

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:40.400 align:middle line:84%
if you were showing
me pictures of them.

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:42.250 align:middle line:84%
And so you might
think if there was

00:07:42.250 --> 00:07:46.480 align:middle line:84%
Bertrand competition
between Mercury and Chevy,

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:49.540 align:middle line:84%
the price of the Bel
Air and the Montclair,

00:07:49.540 --> 00:07:51.880 align:middle line:84%
they could be very
tough competition

00:07:51.880 --> 00:07:54.640 align:middle line:84%
and drive prices
down towards cost.

00:07:54.640 --> 00:08:02.330 align:middle line:84%
So just, Bresnahan has these
pictures illustrating this.

00:08:02.330 --> 00:08:04.210 align:middle line:84%
He supposes that,
you know, suppose

00:08:04.210 --> 00:08:07.930 align:middle line:84%
that this was just a pure one
dimensional vertical competition

00:08:07.930 --> 00:08:09.980 align:middle line:84%
model like I
described last time.

00:08:09.980 --> 00:08:13.240 align:middle line:90%
So we put quality on the x-axis.

00:08:13.240 --> 00:08:16.490 align:middle line:84%
This solid line is
supposed to represent cost.

00:08:16.490 --> 00:08:19.180 align:middle line:84%
And then these are the
prices of the different car

00:08:19.180 --> 00:08:23.510 align:middle line:84%
models where General Motors and
Ford are the two firms producing

00:08:23.510 --> 00:08:24.010 align:middle line:90%
them.

00:08:24.010 --> 00:08:26.320 align:middle line:84%
It is true that
generally, General Motors

00:08:26.320 --> 00:08:28.390 align:middle line:84%
tend to produce the bigger
cars and Ford tended

00:08:28.390 --> 00:08:29.750 align:middle line:90%
to produce the smaller cars.

00:08:29.750 --> 00:08:32.230 align:middle line:84%
So GM, with its
Cadillac division,

00:08:32.230 --> 00:08:34.690 align:middle line:84%
had a lot of the upper end
of the quality spectrum

00:08:34.690 --> 00:08:37.396 align:middle line:90%
more to itself.

00:08:37.396 --> 00:08:41.460 align:middle line:84%
And what he says is that in this
vertical differentiation model,

00:08:41.460 --> 00:08:44.730 align:middle line:84%
if you have models that
are relatively far apart,

00:08:44.730 --> 00:08:47.190 align:middle line:84%
like models four
and five, if you

00:08:47.190 --> 00:08:51.240 align:middle line:84%
view these as very
different in quality space,

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:54.720 align:middle line:84%
then if these are
the collusive prices

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:56.520 align:middle line:84%
and they start competing
with each other,

00:08:56.520 --> 00:08:58.800 align:middle line:84%
it may be that prices
for four and five

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:01.260 align:middle line:84%
don't drop very much because
they're still far enough

00:09:01.260 --> 00:09:04.560 align:middle line:84%
apart that the marginal
consumers are small

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:07.350 align:middle line:84%
relative to total demand so you
don't do much to try to attract

00:09:07.350 --> 00:09:10.290 align:middle line:90%
more marginal consumers.

00:09:10.290 --> 00:09:14.040 align:middle line:84%
But on the other hand, if you
look at models two and three

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:15.750 align:middle line:90%
that are very similar--

00:09:15.750 --> 00:09:20.070 align:middle line:84%
so this could be my
Bel Air and Montclair.

00:09:20.070 --> 00:09:23.548 align:middle line:84%
If they're colluding, they
may charge fairly high prices.

00:09:23.548 --> 00:09:25.590 align:middle line:84%
And then if they start
competing with each other,

00:09:25.590 --> 00:09:28.200 align:middle line:84%
prices may get driven
very low because they're

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:29.435 align:middle line:90%
very close in product space.

00:09:29.435 --> 00:09:35.240 align:middle line:90%


00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:37.590 align:middle line:84%
And so if you had
that as an idea

00:09:37.590 --> 00:09:41.255 align:middle line:84%
and you thought about how you
could test that, Bresnahan could

00:09:41.255 --> 00:09:42.630 align:middle line:84%
have taken this
sort of, what I'd

00:09:42.630 --> 00:09:44.610 align:middle line:84%
call the classic reduced
form approach in IO,

00:09:44.610 --> 00:09:47.560 align:middle line:84%
which is you make predictions,
you test the predictions.

00:09:47.560 --> 00:09:50.070 align:middle line:84%
And so what you could have
looked for is, you know,

00:09:50.070 --> 00:09:53.970 align:middle line:84%
are price drops
from 1954 to 1955

00:09:53.970 --> 00:09:57.760 align:middle line:84%
larger for car models that
are closer in product space?

00:09:57.760 --> 00:09:59.940 align:middle line:84%
And that would be an
auxiliary prediction

00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:03.660 align:middle line:90%
of a breakdown in collusion.

00:10:03.660 --> 00:10:08.580 align:middle line:84%
But, you know, Bresnahan thought
that this is really not all

00:10:08.580 --> 00:10:10.230 align:middle line:90%
you could do with that theory.

00:10:10.230 --> 00:10:14.220 align:middle line:84%
One thing is that the theory
is more complicated than that.

00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:17.970 align:middle line:84%
Like if you remember the
vertical competition model,

00:10:17.970 --> 00:10:20.760 align:middle line:84%
every firm does have a
first order condition that's

00:10:20.760 --> 00:10:23.850 align:middle line:84%
pinned down to the firm
above and to the firm below,

00:10:23.850 --> 00:10:25.350 align:middle line:84%
and those two first
order conditions

00:10:25.350 --> 00:10:26.590 align:middle line:84%
are going to pin
down the prices.

00:10:26.590 --> 00:10:28.132 align:middle line:84%
But those things
are going to involve

00:10:28.132 --> 00:10:31.380 align:middle line:84%
the prices of the next lower
quality model and the model

00:10:31.380 --> 00:10:33.320 align:middle line:84%
below that and the
model below that.

00:10:33.320 --> 00:10:36.380 align:middle line:84%
And so it is a system of n
equations and n unknowns,

00:10:36.380 --> 00:10:39.730 align:middle line:84%
and any change in
the competition--

00:10:39.730 --> 00:10:41.650 align:middle line:84%
like the change in
any one place is

00:10:41.650 --> 00:10:44.542 align:middle line:84%
going to have ripple effects
throughout the distribution

00:10:44.542 --> 00:10:46.750 align:middle line:84%
and will change the prices
of the higher price models

00:10:46.750 --> 00:10:50.047 align:middle line:84%
even if they're not in
very close competition.

00:10:50.047 --> 00:10:51.880 align:middle line:84%
And then the other thing
that Bresnahan said

00:10:51.880 --> 00:10:54.880 align:middle line:84%
is that our theories are
quantitative theories, not just

00:10:54.880 --> 00:10:55.880 align:middle line:90%
qualitative theories.

00:10:55.880 --> 00:10:58.430 align:middle line:84%
They don't just predict
that prices will go down.

00:10:58.430 --> 00:11:01.570 align:middle line:84%
They really are predicting how
much the prices would go down,

00:11:01.570 --> 00:11:05.020 align:middle line:84%
and how much the price would go
down depend on things that we,

00:11:05.020 --> 00:11:06.790 align:middle line:90%
in theory, could measure.

00:11:06.790 --> 00:11:09.970 align:middle line:84%
And so really, to give a more
complete test of the theory,

00:11:09.970 --> 00:11:12.930 align:middle line:84%
we should really see whether
the theory can quantitatively

00:11:12.930 --> 00:11:15.430 align:middle line:84%
account for everything instead
of just qualitatively account

00:11:15.430 --> 00:11:17.440 align:middle line:90%
for everything.

00:11:17.440 --> 00:11:19.070 align:middle line:90%
So what did Bresnahan do?

00:11:19.070 --> 00:11:21.610 align:middle line:84%
And this is where the paper
starts to look awfully primitive

00:11:21.610 --> 00:11:23.740 align:middle line:90%
these days.

00:11:23.740 --> 00:11:27.700 align:middle line:84%
He basically wrote down
the standard vertical

00:11:27.700 --> 00:11:30.940 align:middle line:84%
differentiation model that
I described in lecture.

00:11:30.940 --> 00:11:36.580 align:middle line:84%
So suppose there are n models
with qualities v1 through vn,

00:11:36.580 --> 00:11:40.060 align:middle line:84%
and suppose that
the quality of a car

00:11:40.060 --> 00:11:43.900 align:middle line:84%
is simply a function of
observable characteristics.

00:11:43.900 --> 00:11:46.750 align:middle line:84%
What function of
observable characteristics?

00:11:46.750 --> 00:11:50.920 align:middle line:84%
Basically it's just x times
square root of x times beta,

00:11:50.920 --> 00:11:54.940 align:middle line:84%
where x would contain things
like the horsepower of the car,

00:11:54.940 --> 00:11:56.920 align:middle line:84%
whether it had a
V8 engine, the size

00:11:56.920 --> 00:11:59.110 align:middle line:84%
in cubic inches, the
weight of the car,

00:11:59.110 --> 00:12:02.890 align:middle line:84%
whether it had air
conditioning, things like that.

00:12:02.890 --> 00:12:05.050 align:middle line:84%
He then assumes
that there's, again,

00:12:05.050 --> 00:12:08.080 align:middle line:84%
just like in the vertical
differentiation model,

00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:11.500 align:middle line:84%
there's a mass of consumers
with types theta distributed

00:12:11.500 --> 00:12:14.140 align:middle line:84%
uniform on some
interval zero to vmax,

00:12:14.140 --> 00:12:18.170 align:middle line:84%
and there's a density
delta on that interval.

00:12:18.170 --> 00:12:20.450 align:middle line:84%
And then he says that the
utility of a type theta

00:12:20.450 --> 00:12:25.310 align:middle line:84%
consumer from buying model i is
going to be theta vi minus pi.

00:12:25.310 --> 00:12:27.320 align:middle line:90%
So pure vertical model.

00:12:27.320 --> 00:12:30.980 align:middle line:84%
And then he has the cost of
producing q units at quality v

00:12:30.980 --> 00:12:34.460 align:middle line:84%
is some constant or some
function of v that's

00:12:34.460 --> 00:12:36.110 align:middle line:84%
going to end up
being irrelevant,

00:12:36.110 --> 00:12:39.880 align:middle line:90%
and then q mu e to the v power.

00:12:39.880 --> 00:12:46.160 align:middle line:84%
So costs, again-- v somehow
is like the square root

00:12:46.160 --> 00:12:51.200 align:middle line:84%
of the observable variables and
then costs are exponentially

00:12:51.200 --> 00:12:54.290 align:middle line:84%
increasing in v as you
try to raise quality,

00:12:54.290 --> 00:12:56.180 align:middle line:90%
and then there is--

00:12:56.180 --> 00:12:58.310 align:middle line:90%
it's a constant marginal cost.

00:12:58.310 --> 00:13:01.580 align:middle line:84%
So mu times e to the
v is the marginal cost

00:13:01.580 --> 00:13:02.815 align:middle line:90%
of producing a single car.

00:13:02.815 --> 00:13:06.060 align:middle line:90%


00:13:06.060 --> 00:13:08.880 align:middle line:84%
But then what
Bresnahan's thought was,

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:12.430 align:middle line:84%
is that this is a model
that I can fully specify.

00:13:12.430 --> 00:13:14.700 align:middle line:84%
And if you fully specify
the primitive-- if you fully

00:13:14.700 --> 00:13:17.760 align:middle line:84%
specify the primitives,
which is the production

00:13:17.760 --> 00:13:21.780 align:middle line:84%
functions of the firms, the
preferences of the consumers,

00:13:21.780 --> 00:13:25.440 align:middle line:84%
and the model of firm
behavior, then you

00:13:25.440 --> 00:13:27.540 align:middle line:84%
can fully predict
what's going to happen.

00:13:27.540 --> 00:13:30.930 align:middle line:84%
So basically once you fix
the parameters, beta mu

00:13:30.930 --> 00:13:33.510 align:middle line:84%
and the production
function and delta in vmax,

00:13:33.510 --> 00:13:37.110 align:middle line:84%
in the consumer
preferences, you can fully

00:13:37.110 --> 00:13:39.900 align:middle line:84%
predict what's going to happen
under any model of behavior.

00:13:39.900 --> 00:13:42.270 align:middle line:84%
And so in particular,
collusive pricing

00:13:42.270 --> 00:13:46.320 align:middle line:84%
just becomes a multi-product
monopoly model that we covered.

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:50.520 align:middle line:84%
Static competition would
be another natural model,

00:13:50.520 --> 00:13:53.400 align:middle line:84%
what people often apply
to a situation like this.

00:13:53.400 --> 00:13:57.210 align:middle line:84%
And then Bresnahan also
considers two other models.

00:13:57.210 --> 00:14:01.260 align:middle line:84%
One of these is like
non-cooperative static

00:14:01.260 --> 00:14:03.010 align:middle line:84%
competition between
all products.

00:14:03.010 --> 00:14:05.220 align:middle line:84%
So this is as if
every Ford model

00:14:05.220 --> 00:14:08.380 align:middle line:84%
is run by a different manager
who's competing aggressively

00:14:08.380 --> 00:14:10.340 align:middle line:84%
against every
other Ford product.

00:14:10.340 --> 00:14:13.870 align:middle line:84%
So every product
in the market is

00:14:13.870 --> 00:14:17.860 align:middle line:84%
run by a different manager
who's profit maximizing solely

00:14:17.860 --> 00:14:19.220 align:middle line:90%
based on that car.

00:14:19.220 --> 00:14:21.127 align:middle line:90%
And then he's got a fourth one.

00:14:21.127 --> 00:14:23.710 align:middle line:84%
This is one of those ones where
it's based on somebody's paper

00:14:23.710 --> 00:14:25.900 align:middle line:84%
from the 1970s, and
I have a hard time--

00:14:25.900 --> 00:14:27.460 align:middle line:84%
it's sort of
perfect competition.

00:14:27.460 --> 00:14:29.440 align:middle line:84%
You have a hard time making
out today what is that model

00:14:29.440 --> 00:14:31.232 align:middle line:84%
and what would be the
justification for it.

00:14:31.232 --> 00:14:32.740 align:middle line:84%
But he's got four
different models.

00:14:32.740 --> 00:14:34.615 align:middle line:84%
He notes that under any
of those four models,

00:14:34.615 --> 00:14:36.730 align:middle line:84%
you can work out what
should happen given

00:14:36.730 --> 00:14:40.450 align:middle line:84%
any set of parameters, so then
you can invert that relationship

00:14:40.450 --> 00:14:45.350 align:middle line:84%
and say, given any
one of these models

00:14:45.350 --> 00:14:47.720 align:middle line:84%
and one of these
models applied to any--

00:14:47.720 --> 00:14:52.700 align:middle line:84%
for any behavioral model in
any year, 1954, '55, or '56,

00:14:52.700 --> 00:14:55.100 align:middle line:84%
I can figure out what
would be the parameters

00:14:55.100 --> 00:14:57.210 align:middle line:90%
that best explain the data.

00:14:57.210 --> 00:15:02.020 align:middle line:84%
And then Bresnahan's idea was
for each model of firm behavior

00:15:02.020 --> 00:15:05.470 align:middle line:84%
in each year, I figure out what
parameters best explain the data

00:15:05.470 --> 00:15:07.770 align:middle line:84%
and then ask how well
does that model explain

00:15:07.770 --> 00:15:09.840 align:middle line:90%
the data with those parameters?

00:15:09.840 --> 00:15:12.060 align:middle line:84%
And then I pick
out whichever model

00:15:12.060 --> 00:15:17.010 align:middle line:84%
fits best in every year to say
this is how the firms behaved

00:15:17.010 --> 00:15:17.660 align:middle line:90%
in that year.

00:15:17.660 --> 00:15:20.226 align:middle line:90%


00:15:20.226 --> 00:15:23.160 align:middle line:84%
And the way he does this is--
again, this seems archaic,

00:15:23.160 --> 00:15:27.960 align:middle line:84%
but it's a set of non-nested
hypothesis tests where he looks

00:15:27.960 --> 00:15:29.880 align:middle line:90%
at how well the--

00:15:29.880 --> 00:15:32.110 align:middle line:84%
looks at the model, fits
the model to the data,

00:15:32.110 --> 00:15:34.380 align:middle line:84%
and then, in a likelihood
ratio sense, says,

00:15:34.380 --> 00:15:37.980 align:middle line:84%
do the predictions of the other
model help explain the errors?

00:15:37.980 --> 00:15:39.690 align:middle line:84%
Do the predictions
of model two help

00:15:39.690 --> 00:15:41.650 align:middle line:90%
explain the errors in model one?

00:15:41.650 --> 00:15:43.320 align:middle line:84%
And if predictions
of model two help

00:15:43.320 --> 00:15:47.370 align:middle line:84%
explain the errors of
model one, then model one

00:15:47.370 --> 00:15:49.230 align:middle line:84%
is rejected by model
two and model one

00:15:49.230 --> 00:15:50.740 align:middle line:90%
is not the correct model.

00:15:50.740 --> 00:15:54.300 align:middle line:84%
And so what he looks for is,
is there one of these four

00:15:54.300 --> 00:15:56.220 align:middle line:84%
models-- in each
year, year by year,

00:15:56.220 --> 00:15:57.870 align:middle line:84%
is there one of
these four models

00:15:57.870 --> 00:16:01.736 align:middle line:84%
that's not rejected
by the other model?

00:16:01.736 --> 00:16:05.070 align:middle line:84%
And then the other thing that
Bresnahan really also pioneered,

00:16:05.070 --> 00:16:06.930 align:middle line:84%
and this was a
paper in his thesis,

00:16:06.930 --> 00:16:09.070 align:middle line:84%
was the idea of
a counterfactual.

00:16:09.070 --> 00:16:12.600 align:middle line:84%
His thought was
that if we do this

00:16:12.600 --> 00:16:15.150 align:middle line:84%
and we figure out what is the
correct model in each year,

00:16:15.150 --> 00:16:17.610 align:middle line:84%
then I can do a
counterfactual and predict

00:16:17.610 --> 00:16:19.990 align:middle line:84%
what would have happened
under any other model

00:16:19.990 --> 00:16:21.620 align:middle line:90%
or any other government policy.

00:16:21.620 --> 00:16:24.760 align:middle line:84%
I could tell you what if the
government had had a 5% sales

00:16:24.760 --> 00:16:28.360 align:middle line:84%
tax on cars, what would happen
if the firms had colluded

00:16:28.360 --> 00:16:31.300 align:middle line:84%
instead of competed with each
other, what would have happened

00:16:31.300 --> 00:16:31.990 align:middle line:90%
in--

00:16:31.990 --> 00:16:34.960 align:middle line:84%
any other thing change you
can think of to the industry,

00:16:34.960 --> 00:16:38.590 align:middle line:84%
you can predict what would
have happened under his model,

00:16:38.590 --> 00:16:40.910 align:middle line:84%
and it gives you the
counterfactual prediction.

00:16:40.910 --> 00:16:45.890 align:middle line:90%


00:16:45.890 --> 00:16:50.060 align:middle line:84%
OK, so what are the main
applied results of the paper?

00:16:50.060 --> 00:16:55.760 align:middle line:84%
And I always think of it
like maybe someday, someone

00:16:55.760 --> 00:16:57.175 align:middle line:90%
will rewrite this paper.

00:16:57.175 --> 00:16:58.550 align:middle line:84%
Obviously this is
primitive data,

00:16:58.550 --> 00:17:00.060 align:middle line:84%
like minimal data
from the 1950s.

00:17:00.060 --> 00:17:01.602 align:middle line:84%
Maybe someone will
rewrite this paper

00:17:01.602 --> 00:17:04.069 align:middle line:84%
and say, from a more
modern perspective, what

00:17:04.069 --> 00:17:05.339 align:middle line:90%
do we think actually happened?

00:17:05.339 --> 00:17:07.770 align:middle line:90%
But what does this analysis say?

00:17:07.770 --> 00:17:10.790 align:middle line:84%
First one says that the
collusive model fits the data

00:17:10.790 --> 00:17:15.890 align:middle line:84%
best in 1954 and 1956, and
the static competition model

00:17:15.890 --> 00:17:18.960 align:middle line:90%
fits the data best in 1955.

00:17:18.960 --> 00:17:21.060 align:middle line:84%
So that's his evidence
for his main hypothesis

00:17:21.060 --> 00:17:23.339 align:middle line:84%
that there was this
breakdown in collusion

00:17:23.339 --> 00:17:26.810 align:middle line:84%
and the collusive model fits
the patterns best in '54, '56,

00:17:26.810 --> 00:17:29.640 align:middle line:90%
the competitive model in '55.

00:17:29.640 --> 00:17:34.320 align:middle line:84%
Further supporting that is his
model has a bunch of parameters,

00:17:34.320 --> 00:17:36.270 align:middle line:84%
the mu and the
delta and the vmax,

00:17:36.270 --> 00:17:38.520 align:middle line:84%
and especially the
betas that talk about--

00:17:38.520 --> 00:17:42.720 align:middle line:84%
which relate to how valuable
is horsepower, how valuable

00:17:42.720 --> 00:17:45.810 align:middle line:84%
is air conditioning,
things like that.

00:17:45.810 --> 00:17:50.010 align:middle line:84%
What he notes is that if you
estimate the collusive model

00:17:50.010 --> 00:17:53.850 align:middle line:84%
in '54 and '56 and then
estimate the static competition

00:17:53.850 --> 00:17:57.780 align:middle line:84%
model in '55, the
parameters like the betas

00:17:57.780 --> 00:18:01.930 align:middle line:84%
turn out to be fairly similar
between '54, '56, and '55.

00:18:01.930 --> 00:18:03.840 align:middle line:84%
And that's further
supporting evidence

00:18:03.840 --> 00:18:06.810 align:middle line:84%
that consumer preferences
towards horsepower

00:18:06.810 --> 00:18:09.500 align:middle line:84%
should probably be
fairly stable over time.

00:18:09.500 --> 00:18:13.650 align:middle line:84%
And so what he notes is that if
you do this model in these two

00:18:13.650 --> 00:18:15.660 align:middle line:84%
years and the
other model in '55,

00:18:15.660 --> 00:18:17.880 align:middle line:84%
then the preference
parameters are fairly

00:18:17.880 --> 00:18:20.140 align:middle line:90%
similar across the years.

00:18:20.140 --> 00:18:22.900 align:middle line:84%
Whereas if you try
to fix any one model

00:18:22.900 --> 00:18:25.690 align:middle line:84%
and use any one model
to explain what happens,

00:18:25.690 --> 00:18:28.840 align:middle line:84%
the parameters need to
change a lot from '54 to '55

00:18:28.840 --> 00:18:31.090 align:middle line:90%
and from '55 to '56.

00:18:31.090 --> 00:18:32.170 align:middle line:90%
In some sense, you need--

00:18:32.170 --> 00:18:34.150 align:middle line:84%
prices did go down
despite the fact

00:18:34.150 --> 00:18:36.400 align:middle line:84%
that demand is going way up,
and so you need something

00:18:36.400 --> 00:18:40.660 align:middle line:84%
to say that suddenly
cars got a lot cheaper--

00:18:40.660 --> 00:18:42.040 align:middle line:84%
cars got a lot
cheaper to produce

00:18:42.040 --> 00:18:45.100 align:middle line:84%
and people valued cars more
than they used to value cars

00:18:45.100 --> 00:18:46.460 align:middle line:90%
during that one year.

00:18:46.460 --> 00:18:50.260 align:middle line:84%
Whereas if you use this
change in behavior,

00:18:50.260 --> 00:18:53.140 align:middle line:84%
then the parameter estimates
are fairly stable across time.

00:18:53.140 --> 00:18:55.430 align:middle line:84%
And so that's
supporting evidence.

00:18:55.430 --> 00:18:58.280 align:middle line:90%


00:18:58.280 --> 00:19:00.590 align:middle line:84%
Then what's in his
counterfactual?

00:19:00.590 --> 00:19:03.530 align:middle line:84%
The counterfactual
he does is suppose

00:19:03.530 --> 00:19:06.380 align:middle line:84%
firms had colluded
in 1955 instead of

00:19:06.380 --> 00:19:09.380 align:middle line:84%
engaging in differentiated
product competition.

00:19:09.380 --> 00:19:14.510 align:middle line:84%
And what he finds is that
prices would have been 6% higher

00:19:14.510 --> 00:19:17.870 align:middle line:84%
and demand would
have been 11% lower.

00:19:17.870 --> 00:19:23.300 align:middle line:84%
So this was a 40%
jump in auto sales.

00:19:23.300 --> 00:19:25.860 align:middle line:84%
His model is not
fully explaining it.

00:19:25.860 --> 00:19:29.990 align:middle line:84%
His model is saying,
I'm explaining

00:19:29.990 --> 00:19:33.110 align:middle line:84%
a little more than 25%
of the boom in auto sales

00:19:33.110 --> 00:19:38.097 align:middle line:84%
as coming from the
change in collusion.

00:19:38.097 --> 00:19:39.680 align:middle line:84%
So in some sense,
he's already letting

00:19:39.680 --> 00:19:41.030 align:middle line:90%
you know that his model--

00:19:41.030 --> 00:19:42.830 align:middle line:84%
at least the model
as he's estimated--

00:19:42.830 --> 00:19:45.353 align:middle line:84%
isn't fully correct, because
it's not fully accounting

00:19:45.353 --> 00:19:46.270 align:middle line:90%
for the jump in sales.

00:19:46.270 --> 00:19:50.450 align:middle line:84%
Now, there was a 10% jump in
non-auto durable goods sales.

00:19:50.450 --> 00:19:54.440 align:middle line:84%
You would have said just the
background change in US economy

00:19:54.440 --> 00:19:58.470 align:middle line:84%
income or change in income maybe
could have explained 10% also.

00:19:58.470 --> 00:20:00.330 align:middle line:84%
So he's saying his
model is explaining

00:20:00.330 --> 00:20:04.230 align:middle line:90%
part of what's going on.

00:20:04.230 --> 00:20:08.910 align:middle line:84%
But it is a very nice
use of the counterfactual

00:20:08.910 --> 00:20:14.040 align:middle line:84%
to give us a sense of what would
have happened in another world.

00:20:14.040 --> 00:20:17.430 align:middle line:84%
You see every IO paper
now has counterfactuals,

00:20:17.430 --> 00:20:18.665 align:middle line:90%
which I think started here.

00:20:18.665 --> 00:20:23.030 align:middle line:90%


00:20:23.030 --> 00:20:26.563 align:middle line:84%
As I said, if you
read the paper,

00:20:26.563 --> 00:20:27.980 align:middle line:84%
there are all kinds
of things that

00:20:27.980 --> 00:20:30.680 align:middle line:84%
are going to seem odd to
you, like the adoption

00:20:30.680 --> 00:20:32.440 align:middle line:90%
of the pure vertical model.

00:20:32.440 --> 00:20:38.862 align:middle line:84%
Clearly, I don't think
the Mercury Montclair

00:20:38.862 --> 00:20:40.820 align:middle line:84%
and the Chevy Bel Air
are so similar that there

00:20:40.820 --> 00:20:43.250 align:middle line:84%
would be purely Bertrand
competition between them.

00:20:43.250 --> 00:20:46.310 align:middle line:84%
If you thought about doing
that in today's car market,

00:20:46.310 --> 00:20:48.290 align:middle line:90%
there are pickup trucks.

00:20:48.290 --> 00:20:49.940 align:middle line:90%
There are convertibles.

00:20:49.940 --> 00:20:51.020 align:middle line:90%
There are sports sedans.

00:20:51.020 --> 00:20:54.230 align:middle line:90%
There are electric vehicles.

00:20:54.230 --> 00:20:57.080 align:middle line:84%
If you try to put them on a
one dimensional quality space,

00:20:57.080 --> 00:20:59.960 align:middle line:84%
some of them, some pickup truck,
is going to nearly coincide

00:20:59.960 --> 00:21:01.730 align:middle line:90%
with a Honda Accord.

00:21:01.730 --> 00:21:04.820 align:middle line:84%
Clearly, you need
to have some kind

00:21:04.820 --> 00:21:06.560 align:middle line:84%
of horizontal
differentiation as well

00:21:06.560 --> 00:21:08.600 align:middle line:90%
as vertical differentiation.

00:21:08.600 --> 00:21:11.420 align:middle line:90%


00:21:11.420 --> 00:21:13.880 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, a number
of aspects when you

00:21:13.880 --> 00:21:15.890 align:middle line:84%
see how people do
modern empirical

00:21:15.890 --> 00:21:17.520 align:middle line:90%
work that look very different.

00:21:17.520 --> 00:21:22.260 align:middle line:84%
But I think in some ways,
the basic ideas still

00:21:22.260 --> 00:21:23.330 align:middle line:90%
seem very spot on.

00:21:23.330 --> 00:21:24.747 align:middle line:84%
Particularly, the
one thing that I

00:21:24.747 --> 00:21:27.900 align:middle line:84%
think he does better than
many current models is

00:21:27.900 --> 00:21:30.780 align:middle line:84%
he does take very
seriously this idea of,

00:21:30.780 --> 00:21:33.090 align:middle line:84%
I want this empirical--
this structural work

00:21:33.090 --> 00:21:35.940 align:middle line:84%
only works if I choose
the right framework,

00:21:35.940 --> 00:21:38.580 align:middle line:84%
and he's going to come up
with four different models

00:21:38.580 --> 00:21:40.380 align:middle line:84%
and put a lot of
effort into testing

00:21:40.380 --> 00:21:42.790 align:middle line:84%
the four different structural
models against each other.

00:21:42.790 --> 00:21:45.300 align:middle line:84%
Whereas now very
often it's like it's

00:21:45.300 --> 00:21:47.370 align:middle line:84%
hard to estimate even
any one model, so you see

00:21:47.370 --> 00:21:49.050 align:middle line:84%
a lot of papers that
have, here's my one model,

00:21:49.050 --> 00:21:50.520 align:middle line:84%
and I can estimate
that and I can

00:21:50.520 --> 00:21:52.800 align:middle line:84%
talk about how well it
fits by putting up a graph

00:21:52.800 --> 00:21:55.380 align:middle line:84%
and showing some x's and
y's and show the y's are

00:21:55.380 --> 00:21:56.470 align:middle line:90%
close to the y-hats.

00:21:56.470 --> 00:22:01.920 align:middle line:84%
But I think Bresnahan's doing a
very nice job on that dimension.

00:22:01.920 --> 00:22:06.470 align:middle line:90%
So any questions on Bresnahan?

00:22:06.470 --> 00:22:07.250 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:22:07.250 --> 00:22:10.457 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Why would
collusion break down

00:22:10.457 --> 00:22:13.040 align:middle line:84%
for just one year and then all
of a sudden, the next year it's

00:22:13.040 --> 00:22:13.977 align:middle line:90%
back at it again?

00:22:13.977 --> 00:22:14.810 align:middle line:90%
GLENN ELLISON: Yeah.

00:22:14.810 --> 00:22:18.910 align:middle line:90%


00:22:18.910 --> 00:22:21.935 align:middle line:90%
You know, a little hard to say.

00:22:21.935 --> 00:22:24.860 align:middle line:84%
I will, in a couple weeks,
be discussing models

00:22:24.860 --> 00:22:26.960 align:middle line:90%
of repeated game collusion.

00:22:26.960 --> 00:22:29.438 align:middle line:84%
And in models of
repeated game collusion,

00:22:29.438 --> 00:22:30.980 align:middle line:84%
you do get this
phenomenon of the way

00:22:30.980 --> 00:22:34.640 align:middle line:84%
you support collusion
is via price wars

00:22:34.640 --> 00:22:37.973 align:middle line:84%
to punish somebody who cheats
so that as if you're trying

00:22:37.973 --> 00:22:39.890 align:middle line:84%
to collude, you think
that someone is cheating

00:22:39.890 --> 00:22:43.400 align:middle line:84%
on the collusive agreements and
their dealers are charging--

00:22:43.400 --> 00:22:45.950 align:middle line:84%
you've all agreed that
we're going to charge $7,000

00:22:45.950 --> 00:22:48.200 align:middle line:84%
for our Bel Air and Montclair,
and you think the other

00:22:48.200 --> 00:22:50.540 align:middle line:84%
guy's dealers are actually
giving consumers cuts

00:22:50.540 --> 00:22:54.290 align:middle line:84%
and offering it for $6,500
instead of $7,000 even though

00:22:54.290 --> 00:22:55.610 align:middle line:90%
the list price is $7,000.

00:22:55.610 --> 00:23:00.500 align:middle line:84%
You might decide we're
going to have to punish you

00:23:00.500 --> 00:23:03.490 align:middle line:84%
for doing that and we're going
to abandon colluding this year,

00:23:03.490 --> 00:23:05.240 align:middle line:84%
and we're going to all
learn how bad it is

00:23:05.240 --> 00:23:09.328 align:middle line:84%
and then go back to
cooperating the next year.

00:23:09.328 --> 00:23:11.370 align:middle line:84%
You know, so you could
tell some story like that.

00:23:11.370 --> 00:23:15.190 align:middle line:90%


00:23:15.190 --> 00:23:17.498 align:middle line:84%
There's no discussion in
the paper of why collusion

00:23:17.498 --> 00:23:18.540 align:middle line:90%
would have brought down--

00:23:18.540 --> 00:23:20.680 align:middle line:84%
there's no narrative
evidence at all

00:23:20.680 --> 00:23:23.480 align:middle line:84%
of the executives
talking to each other

00:23:23.480 --> 00:23:25.130 align:middle line:90%
and having anything going wrong.

00:23:25.130 --> 00:23:26.470 align:middle line:90%
But it is true that actually--

00:23:26.470 --> 00:23:28.702 align:middle line:90%


00:23:28.702 --> 00:23:30.910 align:middle line:84%
we're going to study several
other papers on cartels.

00:23:30.910 --> 00:23:33.595 align:middle line:84%
Almost all the cartels we
study are finite lived.

00:23:33.595 --> 00:23:35.470 align:middle line:84%
They go on, they're
colluding with each other

00:23:35.470 --> 00:23:36.887 align:middle line:84%
for some number
of years, and then

00:23:36.887 --> 00:23:39.340 align:middle line:90%
it breaks down at some point.

00:23:39.340 --> 00:23:42.370 align:middle line:84%
In part because also
it's illegal, but it's--

00:23:42.370 --> 00:23:45.100 align:middle line:84%
so people are afraid of getting
caught or something goes wrong

00:23:45.100 --> 00:23:47.683 align:middle line:84%
and they can't discuss it with
each other and get things back.

00:23:47.683 --> 00:23:51.830 align:middle line:90%


00:23:51.830 --> 00:23:52.380 align:middle line:90%
Anybody else?

00:23:52.380 --> 00:23:56.290 align:middle line:90%


00:23:56.290 --> 00:23:57.130 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:23:57.130 --> 00:23:58.908 align:middle line:84%
So second thing I was
going to do, then,

00:23:58.908 --> 00:24:01.450 align:middle line:84%
I was going to jump ahead before
I get to Miller and Weinberg

00:24:01.450 --> 00:24:05.470 align:middle line:84%
and just do a half hour, 40
minutes on oligopoly price

00:24:05.470 --> 00:24:06.710 align:middle line:90%
discrimination.

00:24:06.710 --> 00:24:11.590 align:middle line:84%
And I think in Tirole's textbook
and in many other places,

00:24:11.590 --> 00:24:13.090 align:middle line:84%
price discrimination
is typically

00:24:13.090 --> 00:24:15.610 align:middle line:90%
taught as a monopoly topic.

00:24:15.610 --> 00:24:18.520 align:middle line:84%
And I think that was
because historically you

00:24:18.520 --> 00:24:21.520 align:middle line:84%
had monopoly and perfect
competition as the two models.

00:24:21.520 --> 00:24:24.470 align:middle line:84%
With perfect competition,
price equals marginal cost,

00:24:24.470 --> 00:24:26.270 align:middle line:90%
you can't price discriminate.

00:24:26.270 --> 00:24:28.600 align:middle line:84%
But with imperfect
competition, price

00:24:28.600 --> 00:24:29.930 align:middle line:90%
isn't equal to marginal cost.

00:24:29.930 --> 00:24:32.050 align:middle line:84%
There's no reason
why oligopolies

00:24:32.050 --> 00:24:34.240 align:middle line:84%
can't price discriminate
just like monopolies

00:24:34.240 --> 00:24:35.650 align:middle line:90%
price discriminate.

00:24:35.650 --> 00:24:39.460 align:middle line:84%
And particularly, there was an
early paper in this, Borenstein

00:24:39.460 --> 00:24:43.540 align:middle line:84%
and Rose, 1994, JPE,
look at price dispersion

00:24:43.540 --> 00:24:45.140 align:middle line:90%
in airline markets.

00:24:45.140 --> 00:24:49.570 align:middle line:84%
And what they find is that if
you look at airline city pair

00:24:49.570 --> 00:24:52.720 align:middle line:84%
markets, like Boston to New
York, New York to Phoenix,

00:24:52.720 --> 00:24:56.780 align:middle line:84%
whatever, the markets
that have more competition

00:24:56.780 --> 00:25:00.690 align:middle line:84%
tend to have more price
dispersion on the same plane.

00:25:00.690 --> 00:25:03.092 align:middle line:84%
So if you look at a flight
from Boston to New York,

00:25:03.092 --> 00:25:04.800 align:middle line:84%
there are a lot of
people on that flight.

00:25:04.800 --> 00:25:06.480 align:middle line:90%
They all pay different prices.

00:25:06.480 --> 00:25:08.150 align:middle line:84%
And the Borenstein-Rose
finding is

00:25:08.150 --> 00:25:11.690 align:middle line:84%
that the heterogeneity
in prices on a flight

00:25:11.690 --> 00:25:14.240 align:middle line:84%
is actually larger in the
more competitive markets,

00:25:14.240 --> 00:25:17.120 align:middle line:84%
not smaller in the more
competitive markets.

00:25:17.120 --> 00:25:19.290 align:middle line:84%
So that's an indication
that it's not the case

00:25:19.290 --> 00:25:21.650 align:middle line:84%
that competition
that we often see

00:25:21.650 --> 00:25:25.340 align:middle line:84%
makes it impossible
to price discriminate.

00:25:25.340 --> 00:25:27.810 align:middle line:84%
OK, so how would we model
price discrimination?

00:25:27.810 --> 00:25:30.320 align:middle line:84%
Well, let me first start
with third degree price

00:25:30.320 --> 00:25:31.550 align:middle line:90%
discrimination.

00:25:31.550 --> 00:25:36.330 align:middle line:84%
So you have two firms competing
in two separate markets,

00:25:36.330 --> 00:25:39.030 align:middle line:84%
and suppose there's no
cross market arbitrage.

00:25:39.030 --> 00:25:44.460 align:middle line:84%
So this is two firms selling
cell phone service in Boston

00:25:44.460 --> 00:25:46.310 align:middle line:84%
to the same two firms
selling-- or two firms

00:25:46.310 --> 00:25:48.200 align:middle line:84%
selling cable TV
service in Boston

00:25:48.200 --> 00:25:52.580 align:middle line:84%
and selling cable TV
service in Philadelphia.

00:25:52.580 --> 00:25:56.180 align:middle line:84%
You can't get buy
cable TV in Boston

00:25:56.180 --> 00:25:58.390 align:middle line:84%
and then use it in
Philadelphia or vice versa.

00:25:58.390 --> 00:26:02.270 align:middle line:84%
You have to buy the service
at your service address.

00:26:02.270 --> 00:26:04.850 align:middle line:84%
And so you could have these
same two firms competing

00:26:04.850 --> 00:26:07.058 align:middle line:84%
and they're competing in
these two different markets.

00:26:07.058 --> 00:26:09.466 align:middle line:90%


00:26:09.466 --> 00:26:13.130 align:middle line:84%
So suppose that each of
the markets has Hotelling

00:26:13.130 --> 00:26:17.120 align:middle line:84%
preferences and the type theta
consumers in market m get

00:26:17.120 --> 00:26:22.880 align:middle line:84%
utility v minus p price of firm
one in m minus tm theta if they

00:26:22.880 --> 00:26:27.540 align:middle line:84%
buy from firm one and v minus p
2m minus tm1 minus theta if they

00:26:27.540 --> 00:26:28.640 align:middle line:90%
buy it from firm two.

00:26:28.640 --> 00:26:32.253 align:middle line:84%
So this is just
competition on two lines.

00:26:32.253 --> 00:26:36.085 align:middle line:90%


00:26:36.085 --> 00:26:40.530 align:middle line:84%
This is market one and
it has some parameter t1,

00:26:40.530 --> 00:26:48.760 align:middle line:84%
and this is market two and
it has some parameter t2.

00:26:48.760 --> 00:26:51.800 align:middle line:90%
I think I made t2 the high guys.

00:26:51.800 --> 00:26:53.488 align:middle line:90%
OK, so you just have--

00:26:53.488 --> 00:26:56.030 align:middle line:84%
you're competing on this line,
you're competing on this line.

00:26:56.030 --> 00:26:57.950 align:middle line:84%
These markets are
completely separate.

00:26:57.950 --> 00:27:01.750 align:middle line:90%


00:27:01.750 --> 00:27:05.240 align:middle line:84%
Price discrimination is just
like third degree in monopoly.

00:27:05.240 --> 00:27:07.280 align:middle line:84%
It's just two totally
separate markets.

00:27:07.280 --> 00:27:10.150 align:middle line:84%
So if we have competition
here, what's going to happen

00:27:10.150 --> 00:27:13.930 align:middle line:84%
is you're going to get
p star equals c plus t1.

00:27:13.930 --> 00:27:15.340 align:middle line:84%
You have competition
here, you're

00:27:15.340 --> 00:27:21.550 align:middle line:84%
going to get p2
star is c plus t2.

00:27:21.550 --> 00:27:23.420 align:middle line:90%
We just compete on a line here.

00:27:23.420 --> 00:27:25.990 align:middle line:84%
We get Hotelling here,
we compete on this line,

00:27:25.990 --> 00:27:28.000 align:middle line:90%
we get Hotelling here.

00:27:28.000 --> 00:27:30.620 align:middle line:84%
The prices are different
across markets.

00:27:30.620 --> 00:27:33.910 align:middle line:84%
We're discriminating against
consumers in this market.

00:27:33.910 --> 00:27:36.050 align:middle line:84%
The high types end
up paying more.

00:27:36.050 --> 00:27:37.760 align:middle line:90%
What are high types?

00:27:37.760 --> 00:27:41.560 align:middle line:84%
A high type is somebody who
lives in a market where there's

00:27:41.560 --> 00:27:43.870 align:middle line:90%
more heterogeneity in taste.

00:27:43.870 --> 00:27:46.300 align:middle line:84%
So if there's more heterogeneity
in taste in market two

00:27:46.300 --> 00:27:48.760 align:middle line:84%
than in market one, then
people in market two

00:27:48.760 --> 00:27:51.730 align:middle line:84%
end up paying more than
people in market one.

00:27:51.730 --> 00:27:53.880 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:27:53.880 --> 00:27:57.030 align:middle line:84%
The one policy question
you get is, well,

00:27:57.030 --> 00:27:58.680 align:middle line:84%
what would happen in
a market like this

00:27:58.680 --> 00:28:02.850 align:middle line:90%
if we banned people from--

00:28:02.850 --> 00:28:07.050 align:middle line:84%
what would happen if we ban
people from price discriminating

00:28:07.050 --> 00:28:10.740 align:middle line:84%
and say, you have to charge
the same price in both markets?

00:28:10.740 --> 00:28:14.450 align:middle line:84%
It turns out, in this model
you just get an average markup.

00:28:14.450 --> 00:28:18.930 align:middle line:84%
So if instead of being
allowed to price separately,

00:28:18.930 --> 00:28:20.800 align:middle line:84%
you force them to
charge the same price,

00:28:20.800 --> 00:28:29.460 align:middle line:84%
what you get is price equals
c plus 2t1t2 over t1 plus t2.

00:28:29.460 --> 00:28:35.040 align:middle line:84%
This is what mathematicians
refer to as the harmonic

00:28:35.040 --> 00:28:41.970 align:middle line:90%
mean of t1 and t2.

00:28:41.970 --> 00:28:46.700 align:middle line:84%
And basic math fact is
that if t1 is less than t2,

00:28:46.700 --> 00:28:55.880 align:middle line:84%
then t1 is less than
2t1t2 over t1 plus t2,

00:28:55.880 --> 00:29:01.490 align:middle line:84%
which is less than or equal
to t1 plus t2 over 2 less than

00:29:01.490 --> 00:29:03.260 align:middle line:90%
or equal to t2.

00:29:03.260 --> 00:29:07.940 align:middle line:84%
This is called the arithmetic
harmonic mean inequality.

00:29:07.940 --> 00:29:10.700 align:middle line:84%
You can think of this as
this is the reciprocal

00:29:10.700 --> 00:29:13.730 align:middle line:84%
of the average of the
reciprocals of t1 and t2.

00:29:13.730 --> 00:29:16.890 align:middle line:84%
So it always lies
between t1 and t2,

00:29:16.890 --> 00:29:20.100 align:middle line:84%
and it is less than the
arithmetic mean of t1 and t2.

00:29:20.100 --> 00:29:22.337 align:middle line:84%
But just the way
the math works, this

00:29:22.337 --> 00:29:24.170 align:middle line:84%
is the price that you
end up charging if you

00:29:24.170 --> 00:29:26.240 align:middle line:90%
ban price discrimination.

00:29:26.240 --> 00:29:29.670 align:middle line:84%
So what does banning
price discrimination do?

00:29:29.670 --> 00:29:33.140 align:middle line:84%
Typically, it makes the
high types better off.

00:29:33.140 --> 00:29:38.490 align:middle line:84%
Typically it makes the
low types worse off.

00:29:38.490 --> 00:29:41.840 align:middle line:84%
It does eliminate a
misallocation of markets.

00:29:41.840 --> 00:29:44.300 align:middle line:84%
In many models, if you have
a high price in one market

00:29:44.300 --> 00:29:46.010 align:middle line:84%
and a low price
in another market,

00:29:46.010 --> 00:29:47.690 align:middle line:84%
there are people in
the high market who

00:29:47.690 --> 00:29:49.590 align:middle line:84%
would like the good
who aren't getting it

00:29:49.590 --> 00:29:51.410 align:middle line:84%
and there are people
in the low market who

00:29:51.410 --> 00:29:53.410 align:middle line:84%
don't like the good as
much who are getting it.

00:29:53.410 --> 00:29:55.910 align:middle line:84%
That's a misallocation like
there is in price discrimination

00:29:55.910 --> 00:29:56.960 align:middle line:90%
models.

00:29:56.960 --> 00:30:00.650 align:middle line:84%
So banning price discrimination
eliminates that inefficiency.

00:30:00.650 --> 00:30:03.410 align:middle line:84%
That inefficiency isn't
present in Hotelling

00:30:03.410 --> 00:30:05.180 align:middle line:84%
because in Hotelling
everybody buys,

00:30:05.180 --> 00:30:08.260 align:middle line:90%
at least if he is big enough.

00:30:08.260 --> 00:30:13.900 align:middle line:84%
And then welfare
can go either way.

00:30:13.900 --> 00:30:19.235 align:middle line:90%


00:30:19.235 --> 00:30:21.180 align:middle line:84%
Here, again, there are
no welfare changes.

00:30:21.180 --> 00:30:23.630 align:middle line:84%
It's just a transfer
because everybody buys--

00:30:23.630 --> 00:30:25.340 align:middle line:84%
all efficient
transactions take place.

00:30:25.340 --> 00:30:28.220 align:middle line:84%
First best is achieved
in both models,

00:30:28.220 --> 00:30:29.820 align:middle line:90%
so it's just a pure transfer.

00:30:29.820 --> 00:30:32.360 align:middle line:84%
But in general, banning
price discrimination

00:30:32.360 --> 00:30:35.630 align:middle line:84%
can increase total
social welfare

00:30:35.630 --> 00:30:37.450 align:middle line:84%
or it can decrease
total social welfare.

00:30:37.450 --> 00:30:40.800 align:middle line:90%


00:30:40.800 --> 00:30:42.090 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:30:42.090 --> 00:30:46.590 align:middle line:84%
There's a set of papers in
the 1980s noting that this is

00:30:46.590 --> 00:30:48.960 align:middle line:84%
the typical effect of
banning price discrimination

00:30:48.960 --> 00:30:52.860 align:middle line:84%
in oligopoly markets, but the
typical effects don't have

00:30:52.860 --> 00:30:54.360 align:middle line:90%
to hold.

00:30:54.360 --> 00:30:57.390 align:middle line:84%
So there's a paper by
Thisse and Vives, AER '88,

00:30:57.390 --> 00:31:01.110 align:middle line:84%
that makes the point that
allowing price discrimination

00:31:01.110 --> 00:31:04.140 align:middle line:84%
can make all
consumers better off.

00:31:04.140 --> 00:31:08.010 align:middle line:84%
So price discrimination can make
everybody better off because

00:31:08.010 --> 00:31:10.530 align:middle line:84%
of this effect that Thisse
and Vives highlight.

00:31:10.530 --> 00:31:13.120 align:middle line:84%
When you let firms
price discriminate,

00:31:13.120 --> 00:31:16.980 align:middle line:84%
it lets firms aggressively
target a rival's

00:31:16.980 --> 00:31:22.260 align:middle line:84%
natural consumers, and that can
intensify price competition.

00:31:22.260 --> 00:31:25.500 align:middle line:84%
So the simplest example
that Thisse and Vives gave

00:31:25.500 --> 00:31:28.920 align:middle line:84%
was consider again
the Hotelling model.

00:31:28.920 --> 00:31:31.020 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to give
the Hotelling model

00:31:31.020 --> 00:31:32.820 align:middle line:90%
a different interpretation.

00:31:32.820 --> 00:31:38.370 align:middle line:84%
So we have these two firms
and they're competing,

00:31:38.370 --> 00:31:42.280 align:middle line:84%
and the consumers
have some types theta.

00:31:42.280 --> 00:31:48.550 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to assume that
the firms can observe theta.

00:31:48.550 --> 00:31:53.380 align:middle line:84%
So maybe these are two firms
selling lumber to home builders

00:31:53.380 --> 00:31:56.130 align:middle line:84%
and they know where the
home builder's jobsite is,

00:31:56.130 --> 00:31:58.990 align:middle line:84%
and so they know how long
it's going to take the home

00:31:58.990 --> 00:32:01.930 align:middle line:84%
builder to truck the
lumber from their warehouse

00:32:01.930 --> 00:32:04.960 align:middle line:84%
to his factory site so
they know his theta.

00:32:04.960 --> 00:32:06.460 align:middle line:84%
The theta is
determining willingness

00:32:06.460 --> 00:32:10.900 align:middle line:84%
to pay through some means
that's known to the sellers.

00:32:10.900 --> 00:32:15.160 align:middle line:84%
And so here,
discrimination would

00:32:15.160 --> 00:32:18.610 align:middle line:84%
be firms set theta
dependent prices instead

00:32:18.610 --> 00:32:21.890 align:middle line:84%
of theta independent prices
because they observe theta.

00:32:21.890 --> 00:32:22.390 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:32:22.390 --> 00:32:24.400 align:middle line:84%
Well, first, what
happens in this model

00:32:24.400 --> 00:32:29.530 align:middle line:84%
if firms are-- if they're not
allowed to price discriminate,

00:32:29.530 --> 00:32:33.700 align:middle line:84%
then you just get p
star equals c plus t.

00:32:33.700 --> 00:32:37.342 align:middle line:84%
Because if I know the
locations, but I'm not

00:32:37.342 --> 00:32:38.800 align:middle line:84%
allowed to charge
different prices,

00:32:38.800 --> 00:32:42.730 align:middle line:84%
then I just charge c
plus t to everybody.

00:32:42.730 --> 00:32:47.390 align:middle line:84%
What's going to happen if
I now start discriminating?

00:32:47.390 --> 00:32:52.510 align:middle line:84%
Well, if I'm firm one or
if-- let's say I'm firm two

00:32:52.510 --> 00:32:55.600 align:middle line:84%
and I'm trying to sell
to a consumer over here.

00:32:55.600 --> 00:32:59.020 align:middle line:84%
I know that that consumer
prefers firm one,

00:32:59.020 --> 00:33:01.283 align:middle line:84%
prefers firm one's
product to my product.

00:33:01.283 --> 00:33:04.420 align:middle line:90%


00:33:04.420 --> 00:33:07.360 align:middle line:84%
To get that consumer, I'm going
to have to undercut firm one.

00:33:07.360 --> 00:33:10.630 align:middle line:90%


00:33:10.630 --> 00:33:12.250 align:middle line:84%
In pricing to this
consumer, there's

00:33:12.250 --> 00:33:14.500 align:middle line:90%
no product differentiation left.

00:33:14.500 --> 00:33:17.410 align:middle line:84%
We know this consumer has some
value v1 for firm one's product

00:33:17.410 --> 00:33:19.880 align:middle line:84%
and some value v2 for
firm two's product,

00:33:19.880 --> 00:33:22.780 align:middle line:84%
and we're just pricing to that
consumer like in a Bertrand

00:33:22.780 --> 00:33:24.155 align:middle line:90%
competition model.

00:33:24.155 --> 00:33:26.530 align:middle line:84%
So what's going to happen is
Bertrand competition is just

00:33:26.530 --> 00:33:37.540 align:middle line:84%
going to force prices down until
p2 star equals c and p1 star

00:33:37.540 --> 00:33:45.340 align:middle line:84%
is going to be-- so p2 star of
theta is c and p1 star of theta

00:33:45.340 --> 00:33:47.590 align:middle line:84%
is going to be firm
one's advantage, which

00:33:47.590 --> 00:33:51.630 align:middle line:84%
is negative theta
minus 1 minus theta.

00:33:51.630 --> 00:33:58.030 align:middle line:90%


00:33:58.030 --> 00:33:59.710 align:middle line:90%
Minus 1 minus theta.

00:33:59.710 --> 00:34:03.470 align:middle line:90%
So that's 1 minus c plus.

00:34:03.470 --> 00:34:07.090 align:middle line:90%
So c plus 1 minus 2 theta.

00:34:07.090 --> 00:34:09.699 align:middle line:84%
So what firm one
is going to do is

00:34:09.699 --> 00:34:13.449 align:middle line:84%
firm one is going to
price at its advantage

00:34:13.449 --> 00:34:16.360 align:middle line:84%
relative to firm
two, and the consumer

00:34:16.360 --> 00:34:18.860 align:middle line:90%
is going to buy from firm one.

00:34:18.860 --> 00:34:20.840 align:middle line:84%
And so what you get if
you-- over here I've

00:34:20.840 --> 00:34:24.590 align:middle line:84%
tried to graph what is
equilibrium price paid

00:34:24.590 --> 00:34:27.860 align:middle line:84%
as a function of theta, and
all these consumers over here

00:34:27.860 --> 00:34:29.093 align:middle line:90%
buy from firm one.

00:34:29.093 --> 00:34:31.760 align:middle line:84%
As you come to the middle of the
line, at the middle of the line

00:34:31.760 --> 00:34:33.320 align:middle line:84%
they're in pure
Bertrand competition.

00:34:33.320 --> 00:34:35.278 align:middle line:84%
The consumer has the same
preferences for both,

00:34:35.278 --> 00:34:36.770 align:middle line:90%
so they both price at c.

00:34:36.770 --> 00:34:39.929 align:middle line:84%
And then you go back up
here to c plus t at the end.

00:34:39.929 --> 00:34:42.449 align:middle line:90%
So no discrimination.

00:34:42.449 --> 00:34:43.610 align:middle line:90%
Everyone pays c plus t.

00:34:43.610 --> 00:34:46.190 align:middle line:90%


00:34:46.190 --> 00:34:49.040 align:middle line:84%
With discrimination, the
worst case is you pay c plus t

00:34:49.040 --> 00:34:50.810 align:middle line:84%
if someone knows
you're fully captive

00:34:50.810 --> 00:34:52.730 align:middle line:84%
and like their
product by t better,

00:34:52.730 --> 00:34:56.719 align:middle line:84%
and then otherwise you
pay c in the middle.

00:34:56.719 --> 00:34:57.590 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:34:57.590 --> 00:35:00.020 align:middle line:84%
So all the consumers
are better off,

00:35:00.020 --> 00:35:04.760 align:middle line:84%
and they're better off because
the rivals are cutting prices

00:35:04.760 --> 00:35:08.300 align:middle line:84%
to target consumers who
don't want to buy from them,

00:35:08.300 --> 00:35:12.200 align:middle line:84%
and that targeting the rival's
consumers is an intense--

00:35:12.200 --> 00:35:15.140 align:middle line:84%
allowing them to do that
intensifies the competition,

00:35:15.140 --> 00:35:18.990 align:middle line:84%
and that intensified competition
cuts prices for everybody.

00:35:18.990 --> 00:35:21.270 align:middle line:84%
So that's the thought that
price discrimination can be

00:35:21.270 --> 00:35:24.490 align:middle line:90%
a universal good for consumers.

00:35:24.490 --> 00:35:24.990 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:35:24.990 --> 00:35:27.905 align:middle line:90%


00:35:27.905 --> 00:35:33.720 align:middle line:84%
A second insight, though, came,
Ken Corts, a decade later,

00:35:33.720 --> 00:35:36.573 align:middle line:90%
was that that's true.

00:35:36.573 --> 00:35:37.740 align:middle line:90%
But there's a second effect.

00:35:37.740 --> 00:35:40.073 align:middle line:84%
And price discrimination can
actually make prices higher

00:35:40.073 --> 00:35:41.720 align:middle line:90%
for all consumers.

00:35:41.720 --> 00:35:45.200 align:middle line:84%
And it's the thought that
price discrimination makes

00:35:45.200 --> 00:35:47.840 align:middle line:90%
your pricing more flexible.

00:35:47.840 --> 00:35:50.510 align:middle line:84%
One thing that lets you do is
target your rival's consumers,

00:35:50.510 --> 00:35:52.230 align:middle line:90%
and that's good for competition.

00:35:52.230 --> 00:35:54.350 align:middle line:84%
The other thing it
does is let you exploit

00:35:54.350 --> 00:35:56.900 align:middle line:90%
people who are captive to you.

00:35:56.900 --> 00:36:02.300 align:middle line:84%
And so Corts looked at this
specification where if you think

00:36:02.300 --> 00:36:04.960 align:middle line:84%
about it-- so over here,
what I've tried to graph is--

00:36:04.960 --> 00:36:07.700 align:middle line:90%


00:36:07.700 --> 00:36:13.730 align:middle line:84%
what that's a graph of is
what is v, the value that you

00:36:13.730 --> 00:36:16.130 align:middle line:90%
get for buying from firm theta.

00:36:16.130 --> 00:36:19.340 align:middle line:84%
And what he assumes is
that you get value two

00:36:19.340 --> 00:36:21.170 align:middle line:90%
from buying from a consumer--

00:36:21.170 --> 00:36:23.920 align:middle line:84%
so these are value they
get buying from firm one.

00:36:23.920 --> 00:36:27.390 align:middle line:90%


00:36:27.390 --> 00:36:29.200 align:middle line:90%
Your value goes like this.

00:36:29.200 --> 00:36:31.740 align:middle line:84%
The farther your
preferred type theta

00:36:31.740 --> 00:36:36.450 align:middle line:84%
is from firm one's
product, the lower

00:36:36.450 --> 00:36:39.570 align:middle line:90%
is the utility you get from it.

00:36:39.570 --> 00:36:43.170 align:middle line:84%
And then once the product is
too far from what you want,

00:36:43.170 --> 00:36:45.810 align:middle line:90%
your utility just drops to zero.

00:36:45.810 --> 00:36:48.670 align:middle line:84%
So I have this decreasing
distance from firm one.

00:36:48.670 --> 00:36:49.990 align:middle line:90%
I like it less and less.

00:36:49.990 --> 00:36:51.907 align:middle line:84%
And then once it gets
to some point, I'm like,

00:36:51.907 --> 00:36:52.840 align:middle line:90%
I can't use that.

00:36:52.840 --> 00:36:55.320 align:middle line:84%
And then your utility
drops to zero.

00:36:55.320 --> 00:36:58.230 align:middle line:84%
So that's my utility
buying from firm one,

00:36:58.230 --> 00:37:01.150 align:middle line:84%
and my utility buying from
firm two does the opposite.

00:37:01.150 --> 00:37:04.420 align:middle line:84%
It's zero here and then
it goes up like that.

00:37:04.420 --> 00:37:06.360 align:middle line:84%
So these high theta
consumers really

00:37:06.360 --> 00:37:10.100 align:middle line:84%
like and get utility two from
buying firm one's product.

00:37:10.100 --> 00:37:12.150 align:middle line:84%
The guy in the middle gets
utility one from both,

00:37:12.150 --> 00:37:13.270 align:middle line:90%
and vice versa.

00:37:13.270 --> 00:37:15.690 align:middle line:84%
So in some sense,
I've eliminated

00:37:15.690 --> 00:37:19.050 align:middle line:84%
that targeting your
rival's competitors effect

00:37:19.050 --> 00:37:23.070 align:middle line:84%
by saying that anyone who
gets utility from one firm

00:37:23.070 --> 00:37:25.500 align:middle line:84%
gets zero utility
from the other firm.

00:37:25.500 --> 00:37:28.120 align:middle line:84%
So letting them target
your rival's consumers

00:37:28.120 --> 00:37:30.850 align:middle line:84%
doesn't actually
create any competition.

00:37:30.850 --> 00:37:34.420 align:middle line:84%
So what would happen
in this model?

00:37:34.420 --> 00:37:39.790 align:middle line:84%
In this model, if
I ban competition--

00:37:39.790 --> 00:37:41.210 align:middle line:90%
if I ban price discrimination.

00:37:41.210 --> 00:37:41.710 align:middle line:90%
Sorry.

00:37:41.710 --> 00:37:43.930 align:middle line:84%
If I ban price
discrimination, firm one

00:37:43.930 --> 00:37:47.410 align:middle line:84%
is essentially pricing as if
it had a linear demand curve.

00:37:47.410 --> 00:37:49.220 align:middle line:84%
It's a linear demand
curve that cuts off,

00:37:49.220 --> 00:37:52.120 align:middle line:84%
but if you remember, if you
have a linear demand curve that

00:37:52.120 --> 00:37:53.800 align:middle line:84%
looks like this,
even if it continues,

00:37:53.800 --> 00:37:55.230 align:middle line:90%
you still would price at one.

00:37:55.230 --> 00:37:57.890 align:middle line:90%


00:37:57.890 --> 00:38:00.020 align:middle line:84%
If you ban price
discrimination, firm one

00:38:00.020 --> 00:38:02.320 align:middle line:90%
is going to set p star equals 1.

00:38:02.320 --> 00:38:04.250 align:middle line:84%
Firm two is going
to set p star equals

00:38:04.250 --> 00:38:07.220 align:middle line:84%
1, because they're both just
pricing to the linear demand

00:38:07.220 --> 00:38:09.740 align:middle line:90%
curve 2 minus p.

00:38:09.740 --> 00:38:12.620 align:middle line:84%
Whereas if you allow
them to discriminate,

00:38:12.620 --> 00:38:14.550 align:middle line:84%
I do first degree
price discrimination.

00:38:14.550 --> 00:38:16.670 align:middle line:84%
This guy pays 2,
this guy pays 1.9,

00:38:16.670 --> 00:38:19.950 align:middle line:84%
this guy pays 1.8, and so
on until this guy pays 1.

00:38:19.950 --> 00:38:22.820 align:middle line:84%
So basically, I just
raised prices to everyone

00:38:22.820 --> 00:38:25.940 align:middle line:84%
because letting me
price discriminate

00:38:25.940 --> 00:38:29.565 align:middle line:84%
lets me exploit all-- lets me
extract all of their surplus

00:38:29.565 --> 00:38:31.190 align:middle line:84%
when I don't have
competition, and here

00:38:31.190 --> 00:38:32.982 align:middle line:84%
I've designed the
oligopoly so there really

00:38:32.982 --> 00:38:34.340 align:middle line:90%
isn't any competition.

00:38:34.340 --> 00:38:39.080 align:middle line:84%
So I think Corts's basic insight
was that there are these two

00:38:39.080 --> 00:38:42.170 align:middle line:84%
effects and we
shouldn't necessarily

00:38:42.170 --> 00:38:44.240 align:middle line:84%
expect either of those
two effects to dominate.

00:38:44.240 --> 00:38:46.220 align:middle line:84%
Price discrimination
can be good because it

00:38:46.220 --> 00:38:47.570 align:middle line:90%
intensifies competition.

00:38:47.570 --> 00:38:50.210 align:middle line:84%
It can be bad because it
has more exploitation,

00:38:50.210 --> 00:38:52.550 align:middle line:84%
and it's just going to
matter on the distribution

00:38:52.550 --> 00:38:55.820 align:middle line:84%
of relative preferences for firm
one versus firm two's products,

00:38:55.820 --> 00:38:59.720 align:middle line:84%
whether the exploitation effect
dominates and makes prices go up

00:38:59.720 --> 00:39:01.610 align:middle line:84%
or the competition
effect dominates

00:39:01.610 --> 00:39:03.740 align:middle line:90%
and makes prices go down.

00:39:03.740 --> 00:39:06.710 align:middle line:84%
As I said, this
Hotelling model, it's

00:39:06.710 --> 00:39:08.353 align:middle line:84%
like it's an
intermediate effect.

00:39:08.353 --> 00:39:10.020 align:middle line:84%
It's like you charge
intermediate prices

00:39:10.020 --> 00:39:13.515 align:middle line:84%
and you lose the ability
to compete and discriminate

00:39:13.515 --> 00:39:15.390 align:middle line:84%
in a way that makes you
just charge something

00:39:15.390 --> 00:39:17.973 align:middle line:84%
in between what you would have
charged the high and low types.

00:39:17.973 --> 00:39:22.425 align:middle line:90%


00:39:22.425 --> 00:39:24.190 align:middle line:90%
Any questions on that argument?

00:39:24.190 --> 00:39:25.002 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:39:25.002 --> 00:39:26.460 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: He's just
kind of arguing

00:39:26.460 --> 00:39:29.243 align:middle line:84%
that if it's not actually
an oligopoly, it's monopoly.

00:39:29.243 --> 00:39:30.660 align:middle line:84%
GLENN ELLISON:
Yeah, that's right.

00:39:30.660 --> 00:39:34.140 align:middle line:84%
So he made it a monopoly,
but then obviously you

00:39:34.140 --> 00:39:36.540 align:middle line:84%
could have it be an
oligopoly like this

00:39:36.540 --> 00:39:38.348 align:middle line:84%
and you would get
the same results,

00:39:38.348 --> 00:39:40.140 align:middle line:84%
Like if it was an
oligopoly where it really

00:39:40.140 --> 00:39:44.790 align:middle line:84%
was an oligopoly but most of
them got low utility from one

00:39:44.790 --> 00:39:47.070 align:middle line:84%
and high from the other,
then you would still

00:39:47.070 --> 00:39:48.720 align:middle line:90%
get the same effect there.

00:39:48.720 --> 00:39:50.550 align:middle line:84%
But he's arguing
that it's basically

00:39:50.550 --> 00:39:52.050 align:middle line:84%
it's adjacent to
the monopoly model.

00:39:52.050 --> 00:39:53.830 align:middle line:84%
In the monopoly model
it goes this way,

00:39:53.830 --> 00:39:55.990 align:middle line:84%
so oligopoly model
is near that one,

00:39:55.990 --> 00:39:57.950 align:middle line:90%
it's going to go the same way.

00:39:57.950 --> 00:39:58.450 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:39:58.450 --> 00:40:03.370 align:middle line:90%


00:40:03.370 --> 00:40:04.300 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:40:04.300 --> 00:40:07.970 align:middle line:84%
So that was oligopoly third
degree price discrimination.

00:40:07.970 --> 00:40:10.430 align:middle line:84%
What about second degree
price discrimination?

00:40:10.430 --> 00:40:14.350 align:middle line:84%
And I do think that
oligopoly second degree price

00:40:14.350 --> 00:40:17.710 align:middle line:84%
discrimination is a really
important topic because I think

00:40:17.710 --> 00:40:21.840 align:middle line:84%
many, many products
people buy, there

00:40:21.840 --> 00:40:23.640 align:middle line:84%
is second degree
discrimination there.

00:40:23.640 --> 00:40:24.730 align:middle line:90%
You're buying phones.

00:40:24.730 --> 00:40:27.270 align:middle line:84%
There is the iPhone
13 and the iPhone 14

00:40:27.270 --> 00:40:30.780 align:middle line:84%
and the 128 megabyte and
the 512 megabyte version.

00:40:30.780 --> 00:40:35.190 align:middle line:84%
You're buying cable TV and it's
cable TV package one, package

00:40:35.190 --> 00:40:37.230 align:middle line:84%
two, package three,
package four,

00:40:37.230 --> 00:40:39.360 align:middle line:90%
all with increasing features.

00:40:39.360 --> 00:40:41.680 align:middle line:84%
You have competing
firms selling TVs.

00:40:41.680 --> 00:40:46.110 align:middle line:84%
Everyone's got the 42-inch
TV and the 49-inch TV

00:40:46.110 --> 00:40:48.810 align:middle line:84%
and the 55-inch TV
and the 62-inch TV,

00:40:48.810 --> 00:40:52.200 align:middle line:84%
and each of those TVs has the
4k or doesn't have the 4k.

00:40:52.200 --> 00:40:56.340 align:middle line:84%
But you generally see firms
in oligopoly industries

00:40:56.340 --> 00:40:59.670 align:middle line:84%
are selling multiple products,
competing with multiple products

00:40:59.670 --> 00:41:00.320 align:middle line:90%
of each other.

00:41:00.320 --> 00:41:03.450 align:middle line:90%


00:41:03.450 --> 00:41:05.700 align:middle line:84%
So if you're talking about
oligopoly second degree

00:41:05.700 --> 00:41:08.760 align:middle line:84%
discrimination or the cars
in Bresnahan's example,

00:41:08.760 --> 00:41:11.430 align:middle line:84%
like it's Ford is
producing a line of cars,

00:41:11.430 --> 00:41:12.970 align:middle line:90%
GM is producing a line of cars.

00:41:12.970 --> 00:41:15.370 align:middle line:84%
Those cars are competing
against other models,

00:41:15.370 --> 00:41:17.580 align:middle line:84%
and there's vertical
constraints within the firm

00:41:17.580 --> 00:41:20.370 align:middle line:84%
and there's also competition
across the firms.

00:41:20.370 --> 00:41:25.770 align:middle line:84%
So clearly you want to have
vertical differentiation

00:41:25.770 --> 00:41:28.530 align:middle line:84%
and you want to have
horizontal differentiation.

00:41:28.530 --> 00:41:31.050 align:middle line:84%
And then the
substitution patterns,

00:41:31.050 --> 00:41:34.890 align:middle line:84%
there's going to be cross
firm substitution going on

00:41:34.890 --> 00:41:37.840 align:middle line:84%
and there's going to be within
firm substitution going on.

00:41:37.840 --> 00:41:40.500 align:middle line:84%
And it just turns out it's hard
to write down a nice tractable

00:41:40.500 --> 00:41:45.390 align:middle line:84%
model that has the two
dimensions of consumer tastes

00:41:45.390 --> 00:41:49.170 align:middle line:84%
and has both substitution
patterns mattering

00:41:49.170 --> 00:41:52.560 align:middle line:84%
and the binding IC
constraints and the binding

00:41:52.560 --> 00:41:54.990 align:middle line:90%
cross firm constraints.

00:41:54.990 --> 00:41:57.210 align:middle line:84%
So what the
literatures tend to do

00:41:57.210 --> 00:41:59.420 align:middle line:84%
is do a few different
special cases.

00:41:59.420 --> 00:42:03.490 align:middle line:84%
One thing it's done is to assume
independent horizontal and

00:42:03.490 --> 00:42:04.600 align:middle line:90%
vertical preferences.

00:42:04.600 --> 00:42:06.550 align:middle line:84%
There's this
observation that if you

00:42:06.550 --> 00:42:09.310 align:middle line:84%
make horizontal and vertical
preferences independent,

00:42:09.310 --> 00:42:12.250 align:middle line:84%
you get a surprisingly tractable
model that you can do even

00:42:12.250 --> 00:42:14.560 align:middle line:90%
with a continuum of types.

00:42:14.560 --> 00:42:18.490 align:middle line:84%
The other thing that people do
is have discrete vertical types,

00:42:18.490 --> 00:42:21.760 align:middle line:84%
and when you have
discrete vertical types,

00:42:21.760 --> 00:42:24.940 align:middle line:84%
you can sometimes make the IC
constraints, the within firm IC

00:42:24.940 --> 00:42:26.770 align:middle line:84%
constraints non-binding
and eliminate

00:42:26.770 --> 00:42:29.830 align:middle line:84%
the number of constraints
you have to solve for.

00:42:29.830 --> 00:42:31.570 align:middle line:84%
And then a third
approach you sometimes

00:42:31.570 --> 00:42:33.370 align:middle line:84%
see people do is
actually just eliminate

00:42:33.370 --> 00:42:35.470 align:middle line:84%
the vertical differentiation
entirely and talk

00:42:35.470 --> 00:42:38.680 align:middle line:84%
about second degree
discrimination in a model that

00:42:38.680 --> 00:42:41.206 align:middle line:84%
doesn't have vertical
differentiation.

00:42:41.206 --> 00:42:44.080 align:middle line:90%


00:42:44.080 --> 00:42:45.340 align:middle line:90%
So let me go--

00:42:45.340 --> 00:42:49.680 align:middle line:84%
I'll start with just an
overview of what happens

00:42:49.680 --> 00:42:51.130 align:middle line:90%
in this independent model.

00:42:51.130 --> 00:42:54.960 align:middle line:84%
So Stole has a chapter
of the handbook in IO

00:42:54.960 --> 00:42:59.340 align:middle line:84%
that surveys this literature
that's pretty big and seemingly

00:42:59.340 --> 00:43:00.070 align:middle line:90%
general.

00:43:00.070 --> 00:43:01.980 align:middle line:90%
You have these firms j.

00:43:01.980 --> 00:43:03.840 align:middle line:84%
They can produce a
range of qualities

00:43:03.840 --> 00:43:06.878 align:middle line:84%
s at some constant
marginal cost c of s.

00:43:06.878 --> 00:43:08.670 align:middle line:84%
So typically they
actually have a continuum

00:43:08.670 --> 00:43:12.090 align:middle line:84%
of s's they could produce
if they wanted to.

00:43:12.090 --> 00:43:16.470 align:middle line:84%
Consumer i's utility from buying
a good of quality s from firm j

00:43:16.470 --> 00:43:18.700 align:middle line:90%
is theta i of s.

00:43:18.700 --> 00:43:20.440 align:middle line:84%
So this is the
vertical preference.

00:43:20.440 --> 00:43:23.850 align:middle line:84%
High types are willing
to pay more for quality.

00:43:23.850 --> 00:43:27.960 align:middle line:84%
Minus the price
plus the epsilon ij.

00:43:27.960 --> 00:43:33.950 align:middle line:84%
So this is the logit-like error
that we've had in our models.

00:43:33.950 --> 00:43:39.440 align:middle line:84%
And then models assume that the
theta i's and the epsilon ij's

00:43:39.440 --> 00:43:43.970 align:middle line:84%
are all independent, and
theta i is distributed

00:43:43.970 --> 00:43:47.372 align:middle line:84%
f theta, epsilon ij is
distributed f epsilon,

00:43:47.372 --> 00:43:48.830 align:middle line:84%
and they're pretty
general in terms

00:43:48.830 --> 00:43:51.080 align:middle line:84%
of what distribution of theta
is and what distribution

00:43:51.080 --> 00:43:52.950 align:middle line:90%
of epsilons you have.

00:43:52.950 --> 00:43:59.920 align:middle line:84%
So let me define s star of theta
to be the efficient quality

00:43:59.920 --> 00:44:03.400 align:middle line:90%
for a consumer of type--

00:44:03.400 --> 00:44:05.890 align:middle line:84%
that should say
type theta to buy.

00:44:05.890 --> 00:44:06.940 align:middle line:90%
So you have a consumer.

00:44:06.940 --> 00:44:09.050 align:middle line:84%
That should be a
theta instead of a v.

00:44:09.050 --> 00:44:12.700 align:middle line:84%
So if you have a
type theta consumer,

00:44:12.700 --> 00:44:16.420 align:middle line:84%
they ought to be buying quality
that maximizes theta times s

00:44:16.420 --> 00:44:17.250 align:middle line:90%
minus c of s.

00:44:17.250 --> 00:44:19.630 align:middle line:84%
So this is if the goods
were sold at cost,

00:44:19.630 --> 00:44:20.633 align:middle line:90%
what would they buy?

00:44:20.633 --> 00:44:22.425 align:middle line:84%
That's the efficient
thing to sell to them.

00:44:22.425 --> 00:44:25.415 align:middle line:90%


00:44:25.415 --> 00:44:28.550 align:middle line:84%
So just an example of
a model fitting this.

00:44:28.550 --> 00:44:31.540 align:middle line:84%
Consider a Hotelling-like
model, and suppose

00:44:31.540 --> 00:44:35.140 align:middle line:84%
that epsilon i1 is
distributed uniform on zero

00:44:35.140 --> 00:44:40.260 align:middle line:84%
to t and epsilon i2 is just
the negative of epsilon one.

00:44:40.260 --> 00:44:45.690 align:middle line:90%


00:44:45.690 --> 00:44:48.960 align:middle line:84%
A Nash equilibrium of that
model is just pjs is c of s

00:44:48.960 --> 00:44:51.450 align:middle line:90%
plus t for all j and s.

00:44:51.450 --> 00:44:53.685 align:middle line:84%
So if you imagine, it's
as if we've got this--

00:44:53.685 --> 00:44:58.910 align:middle line:90%


00:44:58.910 --> 00:45:02.395 align:middle line:84%
I've got this continuum
of Hotelling lines.

00:45:02.395 --> 00:45:07.420 align:middle line:90%


00:45:07.420 --> 00:45:09.590 align:middle line:84%
So you have these
different quality levels,

00:45:09.590 --> 00:45:12.913 align:middle line:90%
s1, s2, s3, s4, s5.

00:45:12.913 --> 00:45:16.500 align:middle line:90%


00:45:16.500 --> 00:45:23.850 align:middle line:84%
On each line, let's imagine I
charge p star equals c of s.

00:45:23.850 --> 00:45:28.320 align:middle line:90%
So quality of-- yeah.

00:45:28.320 --> 00:45:32.805 align:middle line:84%
Price for quality
s is c of s plus t.

00:45:32.805 --> 00:45:36.090 align:middle line:90%
So imagine you had that pricing.

00:45:36.090 --> 00:45:40.980 align:middle line:84%
That every quality I
sell at the cost plus t.

00:45:40.980 --> 00:45:43.680 align:middle line:84%
Then what are
consumers going to do?

00:45:43.680 --> 00:45:47.440 align:middle line:84%
The markups are the same
for every quality level.

00:45:47.440 --> 00:45:49.770 align:middle line:84%
So when I'm deciding which
quality level to buy,

00:45:49.770 --> 00:45:53.430 align:middle line:84%
I buy the efficient
quality level.

00:45:53.430 --> 00:45:57.660 align:middle line:84%
If I'm a type theta consumer,
I buy s star of theta.

00:45:57.660 --> 00:46:00.690 align:middle line:84%
So all the consumers for whom
s3 is the optimal quality

00:46:00.690 --> 00:46:06.600 align:middle line:84%
are like, OK, I'm either going
to buy quality s3 from firm one

00:46:06.600 --> 00:46:08.710 align:middle line:84%
or I'm going to buy
quality three from s2,

00:46:08.710 --> 00:46:10.110 align:middle line:90%
who do I buy from?

00:46:10.110 --> 00:46:12.570 align:middle line:90%
Well, again, it depends on my--

00:46:12.570 --> 00:46:17.460 align:middle line:84%
now I'm calling this an
epsilon i1 and an epsilon i2.

00:46:17.460 --> 00:46:22.560 align:middle line:84%
But if my taste is I really
like firm one's product,

00:46:22.560 --> 00:46:23.608 align:middle line:90%
I buy from firm one.

00:46:23.608 --> 00:46:25.650 align:middle line:84%
If my epsilon is I really
buy firm two's product,

00:46:25.650 --> 00:46:27.160 align:middle line:90%
I buy from firm two.

00:46:27.160 --> 00:46:30.030 align:middle line:90%
And so it's just the--

00:46:30.030 --> 00:46:32.580 align:middle line:84%
as far as if the
firm cuts its price,

00:46:32.580 --> 00:46:35.230 align:middle line:84%
it's just like the
competition on a line model.

00:46:35.230 --> 00:46:39.310 align:middle line:84%
And if it cuts its price, it
will attract more consumers

00:46:39.310 --> 00:46:40.340 align:middle line:90%
from the other side.

00:46:40.340 --> 00:46:44.710 align:middle line:84%
It would have this increase in
demand one half plus p2 minus p1

00:46:44.710 --> 00:46:48.700 align:middle line:84%
over 2t, but they're just
basically competing on a line

00:46:48.700 --> 00:46:51.020 align:middle line:84%
against the other firm
for this quality level.

00:46:51.020 --> 00:46:53.680 align:middle line:84%
And so the same Hotelling
first order condition applies

00:46:53.680 --> 00:46:56.950 align:middle line:84%
and they charge price
c plus t on each line.

00:46:56.950 --> 00:46:58.630 align:middle line:84%
And so if the firms
are doing this

00:46:58.630 --> 00:47:00.940 align:middle line:84%
and they're playing
Hotelling on each line,

00:47:00.940 --> 00:47:03.100 align:middle line:84%
they're pricing at c
plus t on each line,

00:47:03.100 --> 00:47:06.830 align:middle line:84%
all consumers do buy the
best quality for them.

00:47:06.830 --> 00:47:09.670 align:middle line:84%
And so the result
here is that when

00:47:09.670 --> 00:47:12.280 align:middle line:84%
you have competitive
secondary discrimination,

00:47:12.280 --> 00:47:14.800 align:middle line:84%
secondary discrimination
goes away.

00:47:14.800 --> 00:47:17.740 align:middle line:84%
The firms are not using
different quality levels

00:47:17.740 --> 00:47:19.400 align:middle line:90%
to discriminate among consumers.

00:47:19.400 --> 00:47:21.430 align:middle line:90%
They just are charging--

00:47:21.430 --> 00:47:24.070 align:middle line:84%
it's not like the iPhone
with 512 megabytes

00:47:24.070 --> 00:47:27.380 align:middle line:84%
cost $300 more than the
iPhone with 128 megabytes.

00:47:27.380 --> 00:47:29.260 align:middle line:84%
They give them all
at the same markup

00:47:29.260 --> 00:47:31.310 align:middle line:84%
and all consumers buy
the efficient quality.

00:47:31.310 --> 00:47:35.700 align:middle line:84%
So all of that
quality distortions

00:47:35.700 --> 00:47:37.340 align:middle line:84%
in secondary
discrimination goes away.

00:47:37.340 --> 00:47:41.160 align:middle line:90%


00:47:41.160 --> 00:47:45.230 align:middle line:84%
And I gave this example
in the Hotelling model,

00:47:45.230 --> 00:47:50.523 align:middle line:84%
but Stole shows that this
is really very general.

00:47:50.523 --> 00:47:51.940 align:middle line:84%
So there's this
very general thing

00:47:51.940 --> 00:47:55.300 align:middle line:84%
of for f distributions
of epsilon,

00:47:55.300 --> 00:47:57.820 align:middle line:84%
satisfying some conditions,
industries of epsilon

00:47:57.820 --> 00:48:00.280 align:middle line:84%
satisfying some
conditions, as long

00:48:00.280 --> 00:48:03.820 align:middle line:84%
as the theta and
epsilon are independent,

00:48:03.820 --> 00:48:05.710 align:middle line:90%
this always happens.

00:48:05.710 --> 00:48:08.140 align:middle line:84%
You just get competition
on many lines

00:48:08.140 --> 00:48:10.750 align:middle line:84%
and constant markups
across goods.

00:48:10.750 --> 00:48:15.040 align:middle line:84%
And I think the thing
that I want to point out

00:48:15.040 --> 00:48:19.510 align:middle line:84%
most about this model is
that it seems very general,

00:48:19.510 --> 00:48:22.810 align:middle line:84%
but it's got this hidden,
special, and incorrect

00:48:22.810 --> 00:48:26.650 align:middle line:84%
assumption in it, which is that
the thetas and the epsilons

00:48:26.650 --> 00:48:31.880 align:middle line:90%
are going to be independent.

00:48:31.880 --> 00:48:33.980 align:middle line:84%
And a good example
to think about this

00:48:33.980 --> 00:48:44.510 align:middle line:84%
is think about you and a CEO of
some company flying to Europe,

00:48:44.510 --> 00:48:46.880 align:middle line:84%
and you're told that there
are two flights available

00:48:46.880 --> 00:48:47.960 align:middle line:90%
to go to Paris.

00:48:47.960 --> 00:48:50.375 align:middle line:84%
There's a Delta flight
or an Air France flight.

00:48:50.375 --> 00:48:53.930 align:middle line:90%


00:48:53.930 --> 00:48:58.070 align:middle line:84%
You might say that some
people like Air France better

00:48:58.070 --> 00:48:58.770 align:middle line:90%
than Delta.

00:48:58.770 --> 00:49:00.933 align:middle line:84%
Some people like Delta
better than Air France.

00:49:00.933 --> 00:49:03.590 align:middle line:90%


00:49:03.590 --> 00:49:06.800 align:middle line:84%
It may be that the
preferences that students

00:49:06.800 --> 00:49:09.380 align:middle line:84%
have on Delta versus Air
France and the preferences

00:49:09.380 --> 00:49:11.660 align:middle line:84%
that the CEOs have on
Delta versus Air France

00:49:11.660 --> 00:49:13.050 align:middle line:90%
are uncorrelated.

00:49:13.050 --> 00:49:14.660 align:middle line:84%
That yeah, they're
both-- like, you

00:49:14.660 --> 00:49:16.460 align:middle line:84%
can model them as
uniform on zero

00:49:16.460 --> 00:49:19.190 align:middle line:84%
to t for their
disutility for Air

00:49:19.190 --> 00:49:22.220 align:middle line:84%
France relative to their ideal
airline disutility for Delta.

00:49:22.220 --> 00:49:26.220 align:middle line:84%
But what's uncorrelated
is reasonable.

00:49:26.220 --> 00:49:29.660 align:middle line:84%
Independent is not reasonable,
because how much are you

00:49:29.660 --> 00:49:30.423 align:middle line:90%
willing to pay?

00:49:30.423 --> 00:49:32.090 align:middle line:84%
Those of you who
really like Air France,

00:49:32.090 --> 00:49:34.800 align:middle line:84%
how much extra are you willing
to pay for to go on Air France?

00:49:34.800 --> 00:49:36.480 align:middle line:90%
Maybe it's $20.

00:49:36.480 --> 00:49:37.560 align:middle line:90%
Maybe it's $10.

00:49:37.560 --> 00:49:38.660 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:49:38.660 --> 00:49:43.250 align:middle line:84%
The CEOs, they may pay $5,000
more to go on Air France

00:49:43.250 --> 00:49:46.040 align:middle line:84%
relative to Delta if they
like it, because for them,

00:49:46.040 --> 00:49:48.150 align:middle line:90%
the $5,000 is rounding error.

00:49:48.150 --> 00:49:50.270 align:middle line:84%
And so I think what's
reasonable there

00:49:50.270 --> 00:49:54.880 align:middle line:84%
is the uncorrelated preferences
that the businessmen

00:49:54.880 --> 00:49:56.380 align:middle line:84%
and the students
may have the same--

00:49:56.380 --> 00:49:58.890 align:middle line:90%


00:49:58.890 --> 00:50:04.200 align:middle line:84%
well, same uncorrelated
preferences for Delta versus Air

00:50:04.200 --> 00:50:07.540 align:middle line:84%
France, but they wouldn't
have independent preferences.

00:50:07.540 --> 00:50:13.980 align:middle line:84%
What it really might look like
is you'd have the student line--

00:50:13.980 --> 00:50:19.380 align:middle line:84%
you have the student line
where t is very small,

00:50:19.380 --> 00:50:23.610 align:middle line:84%
and then you have
the businessman line

00:50:23.610 --> 00:50:26.430 align:middle line:90%
where t is very, very big.

00:50:26.430 --> 00:50:29.497 align:middle line:84%
So they have a higher vertical
willingness to pay for the good,

00:50:29.497 --> 00:50:31.830 align:middle line:84%
but then, in addition to the
higher vertical willingness

00:50:31.830 --> 00:50:35.580 align:middle line:84%
to pay, they just have much more
intense horizontal preferences

00:50:35.580 --> 00:50:36.570 align:middle line:90%
as measured in dollars.

00:50:36.570 --> 00:50:40.008 align:middle line:84%
That's not covered by
the independence case.

00:50:40.008 --> 00:50:42.300 align:middle line:84%
Independence would say there
should be one line up here

00:50:42.300 --> 00:50:44.100 align:middle line:84%
like this where,
again, they'll all

00:50:44.100 --> 00:50:47.717 align:middle line:84%
shift from Air France to Delta
for 20 bucks, which is just not

00:50:47.717 --> 00:50:48.425 align:middle line:90%
going to be true.

00:50:48.425 --> 00:50:50.975 align:middle line:90%


00:50:50.975 --> 00:50:54.260 align:middle line:84%
And it's the independence,
in some sense, that results

00:50:54.260 --> 00:50:57.350 align:middle line:90%
in the constant markups.

00:50:57.350 --> 00:51:00.080 align:middle line:84%
So a second way people
model this is just let's

00:51:00.080 --> 00:51:02.250 align:middle line:84%
go with this
discrete type space.

00:51:02.250 --> 00:51:08.160 align:middle line:84%
So there's just two
quantities, sl and sh.

00:51:08.160 --> 00:51:11.300 align:middle line:84%
sl is the coach class seat
where your knees are pressed

00:51:11.300 --> 00:51:13.970 align:middle line:84%
into the seat in front
of you and then sh

00:51:13.970 --> 00:51:17.360 align:middle line:84%
is the lie flat bed
with the nice TV

00:51:17.360 --> 00:51:19.760 align:middle line:84%
and the champagne and glasses
when you walk on the plane

00:51:19.760 --> 00:51:21.470 align:middle line:90%
and everything else.

00:51:21.470 --> 00:51:24.530 align:middle line:84%
And so what you
can think of here

00:51:24.530 --> 00:51:27.050 align:middle line:84%
is model this is
I can model that

00:51:27.050 --> 00:51:32.200 align:middle line:84%
as the vertical differentiation
model where consumers have

00:51:32.200 --> 00:51:35.830 align:middle line:84%
a discrete vertical
type tl or th,

00:51:35.830 --> 00:51:40.350 align:middle line:84%
and then they have horizontal
types epsilon i1 and epsilon i2,

00:51:40.350 --> 00:51:42.340 align:middle line:84%
just like in the
Hotelling model.

00:51:42.340 --> 00:51:44.890 align:middle line:84%
And let's instead then
specify the utility.

00:51:44.890 --> 00:51:48.820 align:middle line:84%
Instead of being theta s plus
epsilon minus p plus epsilon,

00:51:48.820 --> 00:51:52.480 align:middle line:84%
we'll have the theta
multiply s minus epsilon.

00:51:52.480 --> 00:51:54.175 align:middle line:90%
So that if you have--

00:51:54.175 --> 00:51:58.450 align:middle line:90%


00:51:58.450 --> 00:52:00.670 align:middle line:84%
everyone has
horizontal preferences

00:52:00.670 --> 00:52:03.050 align:middle line:90%
at the same intensity in utils.

00:52:03.050 --> 00:52:05.290 align:middle line:84%
But when you convert
the utils to dollars

00:52:05.290 --> 00:52:09.012 align:middle line:84%
by multiplying by this theta,
the preferences in dollar terms

00:52:09.012 --> 00:52:10.720 align:middle line:84%
are much more intense
for the businessmen

00:52:10.720 --> 00:52:12.490 align:middle line:90%
than they are for the students.

00:52:12.490 --> 00:52:17.470 align:middle line:84%
So the theta, the vertical type,
multiplies both the quality

00:52:17.470 --> 00:52:23.050 align:middle line:84%
and the relative preference
of Delta, Air France.

00:52:23.050 --> 00:52:24.070 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:52:24.070 --> 00:52:26.500 align:middle line:84%
So in this model, it's
reasonable to assume

00:52:26.500 --> 00:52:29.870 align:middle line:84%
that the thetas and the
epsilons are now independent.

00:52:29.870 --> 00:52:32.650 align:middle line:84%
So you have this willingness
to pay for quality,

00:52:32.650 --> 00:52:36.160 align:middle line:84%
whether it be quality of the
nice seat or quality of the Air

00:52:36.160 --> 00:52:37.310 align:middle line:90%
France food.

00:52:37.310 --> 00:52:40.300 align:middle line:84%
And then you have an
idiosyncratic taste

00:52:40.300 --> 00:52:43.160 align:middle line:84%
for the Air France food
versus the Delta food.

00:52:43.160 --> 00:52:44.360 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:52:44.360 --> 00:52:46.850 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, so I'm going to assume
for this numerical example

00:52:46.850 --> 00:52:49.460 align:middle line:84%
that the thetas and the
epsilons are independent

00:52:49.460 --> 00:52:53.150 align:middle line:84%
and half the people
are high types.

00:52:53.150 --> 00:52:55.020 align:middle line:90%
So what happens here?

00:52:55.020 --> 00:52:56.270 align:middle line:90%
Well, you know, simple fact.

00:52:56.270 --> 00:52:59.090 align:middle line:84%
I've got conditions
under which this is true,

00:52:59.090 --> 00:53:01.010 align:middle line:84%
but under some
set of conditions,

00:53:01.010 --> 00:53:05.780 align:middle line:84%
what you get is if this is
a line that has length tl

00:53:05.780 --> 00:53:10.250 align:middle line:84%
and this is a line
that has length th,

00:53:10.250 --> 00:53:14.600 align:middle line:84%
you get p star
equals c plus tl here

00:53:14.600 --> 00:53:18.070 align:middle line:84%
and you get p star
equals c plus th here.

00:53:18.070 --> 00:53:18.570 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:53:18.570 --> 00:53:22.830 align:middle line:90%
So on the big line, you price--

00:53:22.830 --> 00:53:24.427 align:middle line:84%
with the big amount
of differentiation

00:53:24.427 --> 00:53:26.510 align:middle line:84%
on the little line, you
price in the little amount

00:53:26.510 --> 00:53:31.070 align:middle line:84%
of differentiation, and all the
low types by the low quality

00:53:31.070 --> 00:53:34.430 align:middle line:84%
product, all the high types
by the high quality product.

00:53:34.430 --> 00:53:36.590 align:middle line:90%
What has to be true for that?

00:53:36.590 --> 00:53:40.760 align:middle line:84%
It has to be that
the th times delta

00:53:40.760 --> 00:53:43.080 align:middle line:84%
s is the willingness
to pay of high types

00:53:43.080 --> 00:53:45.090 align:middle line:90%
to get the high quality product.

00:53:45.090 --> 00:53:48.057 align:middle line:84%
tl delta s is the willingness
to pay of the low types

00:53:48.057 --> 00:53:49.390 align:middle line:90%
to get the high quality product.

00:53:49.390 --> 00:53:52.800 align:middle line:84%
And it just has to be
that the markups that

00:53:52.800 --> 00:53:57.570 align:middle line:84%
result from different
degrees of differentiation

00:53:57.570 --> 00:53:59.928 align:middle line:84%
are exactly between
the willingness

00:53:59.928 --> 00:54:01.720 align:middle line:84%
to pay of the high
types and the low types.

00:54:01.720 --> 00:54:05.640 align:middle line:84%
So here, just in this case,
under those assumptions,

00:54:05.640 --> 00:54:08.485 align:middle line:84%
the IC constraints
are all non-binding.

00:54:08.485 --> 00:54:10.860 align:middle line:84%
There are no consumers who
are indifferent between buying

00:54:10.860 --> 00:54:12.940 align:middle line:84%
first class ticket or
a coach class ticket

00:54:12.940 --> 00:54:15.690 align:middle line:90%
in this model I've written down.

00:54:15.690 --> 00:54:18.420 align:middle line:84%
Students are just in
competition on a line

00:54:18.420 --> 00:54:19.743 align:middle line:90%
to buy the low class tickets.

00:54:19.743 --> 00:54:21.660 align:middle line:84%
These are contrasted by
the high cost tickets.

00:54:21.660 --> 00:54:23.790 align:middle line:90%
It requires a coincidence.

00:54:23.790 --> 00:54:27.900 align:middle line:84%
The coincidence is
that th minus tl

00:54:27.900 --> 00:54:30.690 align:middle line:84%
is in this range that makes
both of the IC constraints

00:54:30.690 --> 00:54:33.600 align:middle line:84%
non-binding and makes the
businessman buy the business

00:54:33.600 --> 00:54:36.815 align:middle line:84%
class tickets, the students
buy the coach class tickets.

00:54:36.815 --> 00:54:41.670 align:middle line:90%


00:54:41.670 --> 00:54:42.750 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:54:42.750 --> 00:54:45.420 align:middle line:84%
This is a perfectly reasonable
model of secondary oligopoly

00:54:45.420 --> 00:54:47.070 align:middle line:90%
price discrimination.

00:54:47.070 --> 00:54:52.420 align:middle line:84%
Again, it does say that
competition determines markups.

00:54:52.420 --> 00:54:55.410 align:middle line:84%
So it is a very different
mathematical structure

00:54:55.410 --> 00:54:58.290 align:middle line:90%
from what you get in the--

00:54:58.290 --> 00:54:59.970 align:middle line:84%
it's a different
mathematical structure

00:54:59.970 --> 00:55:04.535 align:middle line:84%
from what you get in the
second degree monopoly

00:55:04.535 --> 00:55:05.910 align:middle line:84%
price discrimination
model, where

00:55:05.910 --> 00:55:08.670 align:middle line:84%
it's the IC constraints and
the manipulating quality

00:55:08.670 --> 00:55:11.535 align:middle line:90%
to do things.

00:55:11.535 --> 00:55:14.070 align:middle line:84%
It is the case that
you can still get--

00:55:14.070 --> 00:55:15.430 align:middle line:90%
you can think of you can get--

00:55:15.430 --> 00:55:19.560 align:middle line:90%


00:55:19.560 --> 00:55:23.113 align:middle line:90%
I set these sh and sl exogenous.

00:55:23.113 --> 00:55:25.530 align:middle line:84%
It could be that you can get
manipulation of the qualities

00:55:25.530 --> 00:55:27.240 align:middle line:90%
here where firms charge--

00:55:27.240 --> 00:55:30.120 align:middle line:84%
choose a quality level sl
that prevents the businessmen

00:55:30.120 --> 00:55:31.650 align:middle line:84%
from taking-- in
some sense, they

00:55:31.650 --> 00:55:34.410 align:middle line:84%
choose how far apart to make
these lines so that this price

00:55:34.410 --> 00:55:37.290 align:middle line:90%
discrimination scheme holds up.

00:55:37.290 --> 00:55:41.220 align:middle line:84%
If sl were too high, I
increase sl at some point,

00:55:41.220 --> 00:55:42.840 align:middle line:84%
the IR constraint
would start binding

00:55:42.840 --> 00:55:45.450 align:middle line:84%
and the high types would
jump down and start buying

00:55:45.450 --> 00:55:47.570 align:middle line:90%
the low quality good instead.

00:55:47.570 --> 00:55:52.510 align:middle line:90%


00:55:52.510 --> 00:55:54.280 align:middle line:84%
And we say interesting
about this model

00:55:54.280 --> 00:55:56.840 align:middle line:84%
is this model is also a
model of multiple equilibria.

00:55:56.840 --> 00:55:59.170 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes this model
has two equilibria,

00:55:59.170 --> 00:56:02.620 align:middle line:84%
one where the firms discriminate
and one where the firms just

00:56:02.620 --> 00:56:05.280 align:middle line:84%
sell the high quality
good to everybody.

00:56:05.280 --> 00:56:08.950 align:middle line:84%
In some sense, there's
just this complementarity.

00:56:08.950 --> 00:56:12.310 align:middle line:84%
We know these things have upward
sloping reaction functions where

00:56:12.310 --> 00:56:15.760 align:middle line:84%
you tend to-- you only
go one half to one,

00:56:15.760 --> 00:56:19.760 align:middle line:84%
but as your rival raises their
price, you raise your price.

00:56:19.760 --> 00:56:22.240 align:middle line:84%
That same thing means if your
rival is spreading its prices

00:56:22.240 --> 00:56:23.980 align:middle line:84%
for the high and low
quality good apart,

00:56:23.980 --> 00:56:26.783 align:middle line:84%
you want to spread
your prices apart.

00:56:26.783 --> 00:56:28.450 align:middle line:84%
So you can actually
have two equilibria,

00:56:28.450 --> 00:56:31.660 align:middle line:84%
one where the low quality good
doesn't exist in the firm.

00:56:31.660 --> 00:56:35.000 align:middle line:84%
They just compete for everybody
selling the high quality good,

00:56:35.000 --> 00:56:38.710 align:middle line:84%
and then the other where
this discrimination occurs.

00:56:38.710 --> 00:56:40.360 align:middle line:84%
But the discriminatory
equilibrium

00:56:40.360 --> 00:56:41.860 align:middle line:84%
is better for the
firms than the one

00:56:41.860 --> 00:56:43.370 align:middle line:90%
where they don't discriminate.

00:56:43.370 --> 00:56:48.070 align:middle line:90%


00:56:48.070 --> 00:56:50.175 align:middle line:90%
Any questions on that model?

00:56:50.175 --> 00:56:53.770 align:middle line:90%


00:56:53.770 --> 00:56:54.270 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:56:54.270 --> 00:56:56.550 align:middle line:84%
So then the final theory
topic I want to get to

00:56:56.550 --> 00:57:00.120 align:middle line:84%
is a little bit
on add-on pricing.

00:57:00.120 --> 00:57:04.650 align:middle line:84%
So at first let me go
back to Lal and Matutes

00:57:04.650 --> 00:57:09.780 align:middle line:90%
Journal of Business, 1994.

00:57:09.780 --> 00:57:12.090 align:middle line:84%
Lal and Matutes were
thinking of this question

00:57:12.090 --> 00:57:19.210 align:middle line:84%
of, why is it that turkeys
are on sale on Thanksgiving?

00:57:19.210 --> 00:57:20.850 align:middle line:84%
Wouldn't you think
that the one day

00:57:20.850 --> 00:57:23.310 align:middle line:84%
of the year when turkey demand
is a hundred times higher

00:57:23.310 --> 00:57:25.470 align:middle line:84%
than it is any other day
of the year, turkey prices

00:57:25.470 --> 00:57:28.680 align:middle line:90%
would be high instead of low?

00:57:28.680 --> 00:57:33.360 align:middle line:84%
And let's think about
the following model.

00:57:33.360 --> 00:57:36.720 align:middle line:84%
And think of this as a
model of people competing.

00:57:36.720 --> 00:57:41.640 align:middle line:84%
This is competing supermarkets,
and the supermarkets

00:57:41.640 --> 00:57:46.930 align:middle line:84%
are selling two different
products, v1 and v2,

00:57:46.930 --> 00:57:49.750 align:middle line:84%
and consumers actually all
want to buy both products.

00:57:49.750 --> 00:57:51.960 align:middle line:84%
They want to buy
the turkey and they

00:57:51.960 --> 00:57:56.640 align:middle line:84%
want to buy the stuffing or the
cranberry sauce or whatever.

00:57:56.640 --> 00:58:03.150 align:middle line:84%
And so what my utility is, I get
v1 plus v2 minus p11 minus p12

00:58:03.150 --> 00:58:04.810 align:middle line:90%
minus p theta.

00:58:04.810 --> 00:58:08.010 align:middle line:84%
If I am type theta and
I drive to store one

00:58:08.010 --> 00:58:11.420 align:middle line:84%
and I buy both products,
they are at these prices.

00:58:11.420 --> 00:58:18.590 align:middle line:84%
Or I can drive to firm two and
again get v1 minus p21 plus v2

00:58:18.590 --> 00:58:22.310 align:middle line:84%
minus p22 minus t
times 1 minus theta

00:58:22.310 --> 00:58:26.560 align:middle line:84%
if I drive and buy the
products from firm two.

00:58:26.560 --> 00:58:32.260 align:middle line:84%
And then I can, if I
want, split my shopping

00:58:32.260 --> 00:58:36.430 align:middle line:84%
and get v1 minus p11 by
buying the turkey at store one

00:58:36.430 --> 00:58:39.250 align:middle line:84%
and then get v2 minus p22
from buying the cranberry

00:58:39.250 --> 00:58:41.230 align:middle line:90%
sauce at firm two.

00:58:41.230 --> 00:58:45.920 align:middle line:84%
But then I have incurred
both driving costs.

00:58:45.920 --> 00:58:48.890 align:middle line:84%
And so Lal and Matutes consider
this a multi-stage game

00:58:48.890 --> 00:58:52.160 align:middle line:84%
where what firms do
is imagine there were

00:58:52.160 --> 00:58:53.960 align:middle line:90%
per product advertising costs.

00:58:53.960 --> 00:58:55.610 align:middle line:84%
You don't want to
advertise the price

00:58:55.610 --> 00:58:57.237 align:middle line:90%
of every product in your store.

00:58:57.237 --> 00:58:58.820 align:middle line:84%
That's clearly true
with supermarkets.

00:58:58.820 --> 00:59:00.780 align:middle line:84%
You've got 10,000
products there.

00:59:00.780 --> 00:59:02.460 align:middle line:90%
You can't advertise all of them.

00:59:02.460 --> 00:59:05.720 align:middle line:84%
So in the extreme case, suppose
you choose and advertise

00:59:05.720 --> 00:59:09.230 align:middle line:84%
a price of one good,
and then after choosing

00:59:09.230 --> 00:59:12.050 align:middle line:84%
the price of your one good, you
choose an unadvertised price

00:59:12.050 --> 00:59:14.630 align:middle line:84%
for the other good
which is kept secret

00:59:14.630 --> 00:59:17.150 align:middle line:84%
until consumers
arrive at the store.

00:59:17.150 --> 00:59:21.350 align:middle line:84%
Consumers drive to one firm,
incurring costs either t theta

00:59:21.350 --> 00:59:23.160 align:middle line:90%
or t1 minus theta.

00:59:23.160 --> 00:59:25.340 align:middle line:84%
When they get there,
they learn the price

00:59:25.340 --> 00:59:27.590 align:middle line:90%
for the unadvertised good.

00:59:27.590 --> 00:59:29.792 align:middle line:84%
And then consumers,
after learning

00:59:29.792 --> 00:59:31.250 align:middle line:84%
the price of the
unadvertised good,

00:59:31.250 --> 00:59:33.080 align:middle line:84%
they either buy one
or both products

00:59:33.080 --> 00:59:36.710 align:middle line:84%
there or they decide to
drive to the other firm

00:59:36.710 --> 00:59:38.695 align:middle line:90%
and buy it at firm two instead.

00:59:38.695 --> 00:59:41.790 align:middle line:90%


00:59:41.790 --> 00:59:44.070 align:middle line:90%
Proposition first.

00:59:44.070 --> 00:59:46.470 align:middle line:84%
In the above model,
equilibrium prices

00:59:46.470 --> 00:59:51.750 align:middle line:84%
satisfy p1 star plus p2
star is c1 plus c2 plus t.

00:59:51.750 --> 00:59:53.700 align:middle line:84%
Again, this is just the
Hotelling competition

00:59:53.700 --> 00:59:55.980 align:middle line:90%
on a line model.

00:59:55.980 --> 00:59:59.180 align:middle line:84%
In equilibrium, the price of the
bundle is going to be c plus t.

00:59:59.180 --> 01:00:02.420 align:middle line:90%


01:00:02.420 --> 01:00:04.760 align:middle line:84%
But the individual
prices are not

01:00:04.760 --> 01:00:07.190 align:middle line:90%
like c plus t over 2 for each.

01:00:07.190 --> 01:00:13.220 align:middle line:84%
The individual prices are p2
star equals v2 and pj1 star

01:00:13.220 --> 01:00:16.490 align:middle line:90%
is c1 plus c2 plus t minus v2.

01:00:16.490 --> 01:00:21.590 align:middle line:84%
So basically cranberry
sauce is always

01:00:21.590 --> 01:00:23.880 align:middle line:84%
incredibly expensive
on Thanksgiving.

01:00:23.880 --> 01:00:25.700 align:middle line:84%
You know, you have
this advertised product

01:00:25.700 --> 01:00:27.590 align:middle line:90%
and the unadvertised product.

01:00:27.590 --> 01:00:31.040 align:middle line:84%
You show up at the store to
buy the unadvertised product

01:00:31.040 --> 01:00:34.270 align:middle line:84%
and you find that, wow, that
is exactly my full willingness

01:00:34.270 --> 01:00:34.770 align:middle line:90%
to pay.

01:00:34.770 --> 01:00:38.030 align:middle line:84%
I'm basically indifferent to
walking out versus spending

01:00:38.030 --> 01:00:40.130 align:middle line:90%
$11 buying cranberries.

01:00:40.130 --> 01:00:45.160 align:middle line:84%
But in equilibrium,
both firms are charging

01:00:45.160 --> 01:00:46.490 align:middle line:90%
$11 for the cranberries.

01:00:46.490 --> 01:00:48.570 align:middle line:84%
So there's the advertised
price for the turkey.

01:00:48.570 --> 01:00:50.860 align:middle line:84%
The advertised price is
that turkey is a great deal

01:00:50.860 --> 01:00:53.213 align:middle line:84%
and it just is a great deal
reflecting how much they're

01:00:53.213 --> 01:00:54.880 align:middle line:84%
going to rip you off
for everything else

01:00:54.880 --> 01:00:57.130 align:middle line:84%
you buy when you get into
the store to buy the turkey,

01:00:57.130 --> 01:00:58.280 align:middle line:90%
that's a good deal.

01:00:58.280 --> 01:01:01.270 align:middle line:84%
And in equilibrium,
it's all neutral.

01:01:01.270 --> 01:01:03.022 align:middle line:84%
In equilibrium it's
as if you advertised

01:01:03.022 --> 01:01:04.480 align:middle line:84%
the prices for
everything, we would

01:01:04.480 --> 01:01:06.320 align:middle line:90%
get exactly the same outcome.

01:01:06.320 --> 01:01:08.420 align:middle line:90%
It's c plus t pricing.

01:01:08.420 --> 01:01:12.820 align:middle line:84%
It's just in a distorted way
where one good is really cheap

01:01:12.820 --> 01:01:15.860 align:middle line:84%
and then the other
goods are expensive.

01:01:15.860 --> 01:01:19.750 align:middle line:84%
And actually, there's actually
a paper on Thanksgiving noting

01:01:19.750 --> 01:01:22.450 align:middle line:84%
that, in fact, it is true
that turkeys are cheapest

01:01:22.450 --> 01:01:24.530 align:middle line:84%
on Thanksgiving than any
other day of the year

01:01:24.530 --> 01:01:26.230 align:middle line:84%
and everything else
in the grocery store

01:01:26.230 --> 01:01:29.890 align:middle line:84%
is, on average, more expensive
than any other day of the year.

01:01:29.890 --> 01:01:33.610 align:middle line:84%
This is what firms seem
to do is, you know,

01:01:33.610 --> 01:01:35.705 align:middle line:90%
turkeys are a loss leader.

01:01:35.705 --> 01:01:37.580 align:middle line:84%
Actually, why are turkeys
a good loss leader?

01:01:37.580 --> 01:01:40.570 align:middle line:84%
Milk is often also a loss
leader at convenience stores.

01:01:40.570 --> 01:01:42.700 align:middle line:84%
Like a gallon of milk
seems relatively cheap

01:01:42.700 --> 01:01:44.710 align:middle line:84%
compared to a bag
of potato chips.

01:01:44.710 --> 01:01:46.840 align:middle line:84%
Turkey and milk are
good loss leaders

01:01:46.840 --> 01:01:49.900 align:middle line:84%
because you don't want to have a
loss leader where people come in

01:01:49.900 --> 01:01:53.770 align:middle line:84%
and stock up and buy 23 of
the thing that's advertised

01:01:53.770 --> 01:01:57.370 align:middle line:84%
as a great deal and then not
buy anything else at your store

01:01:57.370 --> 01:01:59.292 align:middle line:84%
and exploit you and
save it up and live off

01:01:59.292 --> 01:02:00.500 align:middle line:90%
that for the next six months.

01:02:00.500 --> 01:02:03.290 align:middle line:84%
Like ramen noodles would
be a terrible loss leader

01:02:03.290 --> 01:02:06.650 align:middle line:84%
because you'd get the
student comes in, buys

01:02:06.650 --> 01:02:09.690 align:middle line:84%
the case of ramen noodles, and
just leaves with just that.

01:02:09.690 --> 01:02:13.400 align:middle line:84%
But turkeys, you really
don't want more than one.

01:02:13.400 --> 01:02:16.290 align:middle line:84%
Gallons of milk, you really
don't want more than one.

01:02:16.290 --> 01:02:19.160 align:middle line:84%
So something that you can give
people a very good deal on,

01:02:19.160 --> 01:02:21.170 align:middle line:84%
they buy exactly
one, and then you

01:02:21.170 --> 01:02:22.650 align:middle line:84%
rip them off for
everything else,

01:02:22.650 --> 01:02:25.430 align:middle line:84%
that's how you choose
your loss leaders.

01:02:25.430 --> 01:02:26.240 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:02:26.240 --> 01:02:28.040 align:middle line:84%
But, you know, the
Lal and Matutes model

01:02:28.040 --> 01:02:32.180 align:middle line:84%
says that this is how loss
leaders work, but they

01:02:32.180 --> 01:02:34.440 align:middle line:90%
are neutral economically.

01:02:34.440 --> 01:02:38.448 align:middle line:84%
It's not like banning them
would do anyone any good.

01:02:38.448 --> 01:02:40.490 align:middle line:84%
They're completely neutral
for the firm's profits

01:02:40.490 --> 01:02:42.500 align:middle line:84%
and neutral for
consumers, and they do

01:02:42.500 --> 01:02:44.250 align:middle line:90%
economize on advertising costs.

01:02:44.250 --> 01:02:46.910 align:middle line:90%
So in that sense, they're good.

01:02:46.910 --> 01:02:48.510 align:middle line:90%
I put Carmen's picture up there.

01:02:48.510 --> 01:02:50.080 align:middle line:84%
I respect-- Carmen,
at one point,

01:02:50.080 --> 01:02:53.960 align:middle line:84%
was a very successful
economist and then

01:02:53.960 --> 01:02:55.960 align:middle line:84%
had been a professor at
Berkeley and dropped out

01:02:55.960 --> 01:02:57.100 align:middle line:90%
and became a novelist.

01:02:57.100 --> 01:02:58.840 align:middle line:84%
And actually is a
successful novelist,

01:02:58.840 --> 01:03:01.060 align:middle line:84%
although she writes in
Spanish so I've never

01:03:01.060 --> 01:03:02.150 align:middle line:90%
read any of her books.

01:03:02.150 --> 01:03:06.910 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, won several
prizes for her fiction

01:03:06.910 --> 01:03:11.435 align:middle line:84%
writing after she decided
to have a second career.

01:03:11.435 --> 01:03:11.935 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:03:11.935 --> 01:03:14.620 align:middle line:90%


01:03:14.620 --> 01:03:15.620 align:middle line:90%
Add-on pricing.

01:03:15.620 --> 01:03:21.700 align:middle line:84%
So add-on pricing thinks about
basically this loss leader

01:03:21.700 --> 01:03:26.800 align:middle line:84%
idea in a vertical competitive
price discrimination model.

01:03:26.800 --> 01:03:30.310 align:middle line:84%
So there are many
cases where firms

01:03:30.310 --> 01:03:33.760 align:middle line:84%
that are practicing second
degree price discrimination

01:03:33.760 --> 01:03:36.470 align:middle line:84%
do have non visible
prices for add-ons.

01:03:36.470 --> 01:03:38.650 align:middle line:84%
So for instance,
you go to a hotel

01:03:38.650 --> 01:03:41.350 align:middle line:84%
and it's very easy to
find the price of a hotel

01:03:41.350 --> 01:03:44.150 align:middle line:84%
online and compare
prices of hotels.

01:03:44.150 --> 01:03:47.170 align:middle line:84%
It's very hard to
compare what's the price

01:03:47.170 --> 01:03:49.210 align:middle line:84%
of the beer in the
mini-bar or what's

01:03:49.210 --> 01:03:51.670 align:middle line:84%
the price of the sparkling
water in the mini-bar, what's

01:03:51.670 --> 01:03:53.810 align:middle line:84%
the price of a croissant
in the hotel lobby

01:03:53.810 --> 01:03:57.670 align:middle line:84%
if I'm in a hurry
to get somewhere.

01:03:57.670 --> 01:03:59.530 align:middle line:84%
Rental cars, it's
very easy to compare

01:03:59.530 --> 01:04:00.830 align:middle line:90%
the price of the rental car.

01:04:00.830 --> 01:04:04.360 align:middle line:84%
It's very hard to figure out
what will I pay for the car seat

01:04:04.360 --> 01:04:07.300 align:middle line:84%
if I need a car seat for
my infant when I get there.

01:04:07.300 --> 01:04:08.170 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

01:04:08.170 --> 01:04:10.128 align:middle line:84%
I guess you guys are too
young to have infants.

01:04:10.128 --> 01:04:14.200 align:middle line:84%
But it's striking to you that
you go to rent a car and you can

01:04:14.200 --> 01:04:17.780 align:middle line:84%
rent a $20,000 car
for 19.95 a day,

01:04:17.780 --> 01:04:20.900 align:middle line:84%
and then you want to rent a $37
car seat because you don't want

01:04:20.900 --> 01:04:24.080 align:middle line:84%
to lug it on the plane,
and it's like 13.95 a day.

01:04:24.080 --> 01:04:27.140 align:middle line:84%
And you're like, you know, how
can this be that they can rent

01:04:27.140 --> 01:04:30.860 align:middle line:84%
me a $20,000 car for $20
and then the $37 car seat,

01:04:30.860 --> 01:04:35.030 align:middle line:84%
they're charging me $13 a day
or making me carry this enormous

01:04:35.030 --> 01:04:37.190 align:middle line:84%
piece of plastic
on the airplane.

01:04:37.190 --> 01:04:39.770 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, it's this
same add-on pricing thing

01:04:39.770 --> 01:04:42.320 align:middle line:84%
where it's very easy to
compare the prices of the cars

01:04:42.320 --> 01:04:44.700 align:middle line:90%
and not of the car seats.

01:04:44.700 --> 01:04:47.580 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, the car
seat is priced at v2.

01:04:47.580 --> 01:04:50.730 align:middle line:84%
What is your disutility from
carrying a car seat on a plane?

01:04:50.730 --> 01:04:52.370 align:middle line:90%
That's what they'll charge you.

01:04:52.370 --> 01:04:55.767 align:middle line:84%
And then the cars are, in
some sense, the loss leader.

01:04:55.767 --> 01:04:58.100 align:middle line:84%
They make money selling the
car seats and the rental car

01:04:58.100 --> 01:05:01.280 align:middle line:90%
insurance.

01:05:01.280 --> 01:05:03.480 align:middle line:84%
Inkjet printers-- I
mentioned earlier that,

01:05:03.480 --> 01:05:05.480 align:middle line:84%
again, the inkjet
printer is very cheap,

01:05:05.480 --> 01:05:07.610 align:middle line:84%
but they've invented
this inkjet printer

01:05:07.610 --> 01:05:10.020 align:middle line:84%
that has almost no ink
in it when you buy it.

01:05:10.020 --> 01:05:12.620 align:middle line:84%
And then it's got the
starter cartridge,

01:05:12.620 --> 01:05:15.650 align:middle line:84%
and then the regular cartridge
of the inkjet printer

01:05:15.650 --> 01:05:18.410 align:middle line:84%
is much more expensive, and
that's the hidden feature

01:05:18.410 --> 01:05:22.670 align:middle line:90%
when you buy the cartridge.

01:05:22.670 --> 01:05:27.050 align:middle line:84%
To think about how this works
in vertical competition,

01:05:27.050 --> 01:05:29.280 align:middle line:84%
Lal and Matutes was purely
additive competition.

01:05:29.280 --> 01:05:30.810 align:middle line:90%
It wasn't a vertical model.

01:05:30.810 --> 01:05:33.560 align:middle line:84%
So imagine you have
firms advertise

01:05:33.560 --> 01:05:35.930 align:middle line:84%
prices for their low quality
goods like the rental car

01:05:35.930 --> 01:05:36.895 align:middle line:90%
without a car seat.

01:05:36.895 --> 01:05:40.360 align:middle line:90%


01:05:40.360 --> 01:05:43.172 align:middle line:84%
Costs sl and sh
both have cost c,

01:05:43.172 --> 01:05:45.130 align:middle line:84%
as if the car seat is
essentially free for them

01:05:45.130 --> 01:05:47.650 align:middle line:90%
to provide.

01:05:47.650 --> 01:05:52.880 align:middle line:84%
Consumers incur some cost to
visit j and learn the price.

01:05:52.880 --> 01:05:55.480 align:middle line:84%
So consumers have
to visit the firm

01:05:55.480 --> 01:05:58.785 align:middle line:84%
to learn the price
of the upgrades.

01:05:58.785 --> 01:06:00.910 align:middle line:84%
You have to spend some time
on the website learning

01:06:00.910 --> 01:06:03.700 align:middle line:84%
what it costs to get a car
seat added to your rental car,

01:06:03.700 --> 01:06:06.700 align:middle line:84%
and then that would let
you upgrade in quality.

01:06:06.700 --> 01:06:09.860 align:middle line:84%
And then again, let's do the
competition on two lines.

01:06:09.860 --> 01:06:14.450 align:middle line:84%
So epsilon i1, epsilon
i2, independent.

01:06:14.450 --> 01:06:18.250 align:middle line:90%
Again, same thing, this setup.

01:06:18.250 --> 01:06:21.400 align:middle line:84%
So this set up where what
you do is imagine this set up

01:06:21.400 --> 01:06:24.220 align:middle line:84%
where you're only advertising
the price of the low quality

01:06:24.220 --> 01:06:28.180 align:middle line:84%
good, and then consumers have to
pay something after they arrive

01:06:28.180 --> 01:06:30.520 align:middle line:84%
or after they've spent some
time investigating your firm

01:06:30.520 --> 01:06:35.740 align:middle line:84%
to learn what it cost to upgrade
to the high quality good.

01:06:35.740 --> 01:06:38.530 align:middle line:90%
What happens?

01:06:38.530 --> 01:06:43.000 align:middle line:84%
Well, there's an equilibrium
where, like in Lal and Matutes,

01:06:43.000 --> 01:06:48.850 align:middle line:84%
the price of the upgrade is
the full value for the upgrade

01:06:48.850 --> 01:06:50.550 align:middle line:90%
for the high types.

01:06:50.550 --> 01:06:52.370 align:middle line:84%
So the price of
the upgrade is what

01:06:52.370 --> 01:06:57.950 align:middle line:84%
are the businessmen willing to
pay for the rental car seat.

01:06:57.950 --> 01:07:00.170 align:middle line:84%
And then price of
the low quality good

01:07:00.170 --> 01:07:04.580 align:middle line:84%
ends up being this,
which I won't get into.

01:07:04.580 --> 01:07:08.805 align:middle line:84%
But main applied conclusions
are that profits in this game

01:07:08.805 --> 01:07:10.430 align:middle line:84%
are higher than the
profits in the game

01:07:10.430 --> 01:07:12.328 align:middle line:84%
where pl and ph
are both visible.

01:07:12.328 --> 01:07:14.120 align:middle line:84%
So in this competitive
price discrimination

01:07:14.120 --> 01:07:20.970 align:middle line:84%
model, add-on pricing or these
loss leaders are not neutral.

01:07:20.970 --> 01:07:22.550 align:middle line:90%
They raise prices.

01:07:22.550 --> 01:07:24.290 align:middle line:84%
And not only do
they raise prices,

01:07:24.290 --> 01:07:28.100 align:middle line:84%
but they make all consumers
worse off than in the game

01:07:28.100 --> 01:07:30.680 align:middle line:84%
where the low quality
good didn't exist.

01:07:30.680 --> 01:07:33.410 align:middle line:84%
So if I banned the
production of rental cars

01:07:33.410 --> 01:07:36.500 align:middle line:84%
that don't come with a car seat,
if you ask for it for free,

01:07:36.500 --> 01:07:38.270 align:middle line:84%
all consumers would
be better off.

01:07:38.270 --> 01:07:39.680 align:middle line:84%
The low types
would be better off

01:07:39.680 --> 01:07:43.025 align:middle line:84%
and the high types
would be better off.

01:07:43.025 --> 01:07:44.900 align:middle line:84%
The high types are
better off because they

01:07:44.900 --> 01:07:47.938 align:middle line:84%
would pay a low price without
this add-on pricing scheme.

01:07:47.938 --> 01:07:50.480 align:middle line:84%
The low types are better off
because they're getting a better

01:07:50.480 --> 01:07:53.300 align:middle line:84%
quality good instead of
having to carry their car

01:07:53.300 --> 01:07:56.540 align:middle line:90%
seats on the plane.

01:07:56.540 --> 01:07:59.280 align:middle line:84%
I'm short on time, so
why don't I skip that?

01:07:59.280 --> 01:08:00.950 align:middle line:84%
But let me just give
you the intuition

01:08:00.950 --> 01:08:05.930 align:middle line:84%
for why that add-on pricing
becomes non-neutral.

01:08:05.930 --> 01:08:08.150 align:middle line:84%
Think about reducing
your price to p star

01:08:08.150 --> 01:08:11.870 align:middle line:84%
minus dp in the add-on model
and doing the same in the model

01:08:11.870 --> 01:08:14.450 align:middle line:90%
with no low quality good.

01:08:14.450 --> 01:08:17.420 align:middle line:84%
In both models, what you
lose from a price cut

01:08:17.420 --> 01:08:21.080 align:middle line:84%
is one half dp from charging
the lower prices to the half

01:08:21.080 --> 01:08:23.120 align:middle line:90%
the consumers you're serving.

01:08:23.120 --> 01:08:26.689 align:middle line:84%
In both models, what you're
gaining from the price

01:08:26.689 --> 01:08:30.470 align:middle line:84%
cut is the number of customers
gained times the per customer

01:08:30.470 --> 01:08:34.000 align:middle line:84%
profit on the marginal
consumer that you gain.

01:08:34.000 --> 01:08:38.560 align:middle line:84%
And so because, in both
models, if you tried to make--

01:08:38.560 --> 01:08:41.050 align:middle line:84%
in both models, the
first order condition,

01:08:41.050 --> 01:08:43.750 align:middle line:90%
this has to equal this.

01:08:43.750 --> 01:08:46.510 align:middle line:84%
The one half dp is the same in
both, the number of customers

01:08:46.510 --> 01:08:49.029 align:middle line:84%
gained in both, therefore,
the per customer profit

01:08:49.029 --> 01:08:52.050 align:middle line:84%
on the marginal consumer
is the same in both models.

01:08:52.050 --> 01:08:54.609 align:middle line:84%
So in both models, the
per customer profit

01:08:54.609 --> 01:08:59.500 align:middle line:84%
you make on the marginal
consumer is the same.

01:08:59.500 --> 01:09:04.670 align:middle line:84%
But profits are based on profits
on the average consumer, not

01:09:04.670 --> 01:09:06.500 align:middle line:90%
the marginal consumer.

01:09:06.500 --> 01:09:08.720 align:middle line:84%
And if you think about
what goes on in a model

01:09:08.720 --> 01:09:12.770 align:middle line:84%
like this, when I cut my
price, who do I get by--

01:09:12.770 --> 01:09:16.520 align:middle line:84%
I offer my rental car for $1
less than everybody else's

01:09:16.520 --> 01:09:17.069 align:middle line:90%
rental car.

01:09:17.069 --> 01:09:21.170 align:middle line:84%
My rental car is 38.95 a
day instead of 39.95 a day.

01:09:21.170 --> 01:09:22.939 align:middle line:90%
I get all the cheapskates.

01:09:22.939 --> 01:09:25.340 align:middle line:84%
And I get all the cheapskates
where the low types

01:09:25.340 --> 01:09:27.710 align:middle line:84%
were willing to carry
their car seat on a plane

01:09:27.710 --> 01:09:31.279 align:middle line:84%
and spend extra time waiting at
the gate to get their car seat

01:09:31.279 --> 01:09:35.960 align:middle line:84%
and carrying it through the
airport to save the 13.95 a day.

01:09:35.960 --> 01:09:41.240 align:middle line:84%
And so because the cheapskates
are an adversely selected

01:09:41.240 --> 01:09:43.800 align:middle line:84%
population relative to
the full population,

01:09:43.800 --> 01:09:47.029 align:middle line:84%
so when I cut my price
in this add-on model,

01:09:47.029 --> 01:09:50.200 align:middle line:90%
I get a bunch of cheapskates.

01:09:50.200 --> 01:09:53.033 align:middle line:84%
If the marginal profit--
so in the add-on model,

01:09:53.033 --> 01:09:54.700 align:middle line:84%
there's this big gap
between the profits

01:09:54.700 --> 01:09:56.408 align:middle line:84%
on the marginal
consumers and the profits

01:09:56.408 --> 01:09:58.390 align:middle line:84%
on the average
consumers, whereas

01:09:58.390 --> 01:10:01.360 align:middle line:84%
in the single good product, the
marginal and average consumers

01:10:01.360 --> 01:10:03.890 align:middle line:90%
are exactly the same.

01:10:03.890 --> 01:10:06.320 align:middle line:84%
So what we've done
is we've just created

01:10:06.320 --> 01:10:09.230 align:middle line:84%
this gap between marginal
consumers and average consumers,

01:10:09.230 --> 01:10:13.130 align:middle line:84%
and that ends up raising
equilibrium prices.

01:10:13.130 --> 01:10:16.250 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes you've created an
adverse selection problem,

01:10:16.250 --> 01:10:19.190 align:middle line:84%
and that adverse
selection problem

01:10:19.190 --> 01:10:21.890 align:middle line:84%
makes it hard for firms to price
compete against other firms,

01:10:21.890 --> 01:10:23.780 align:middle line:84%
because if you cut
prices, you just

01:10:23.780 --> 01:10:27.175 align:middle line:84%
get cheapskates on whom you lose
money renting the cars so cheap.

01:10:27.175 --> 01:10:30.410 align:middle line:90%


01:10:30.410 --> 01:10:31.890 align:middle line:90%
Questions?

01:10:31.890 --> 01:10:32.390 align:middle line:90%
No?

01:10:32.390 --> 01:10:34.340 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:10:34.340 --> 01:10:36.380 align:middle line:84%
Leaves me with 10
minutes, and I said

01:10:36.380 --> 01:10:39.860 align:middle line:84%
I would do this paper very
quickly because this really

01:10:39.860 --> 01:10:42.620 align:middle line:84%
follows very much the
methodology Tobias is talking

01:10:42.620 --> 01:10:45.840 align:middle line:90%
about on Monday of next week.

01:10:45.840 --> 01:10:51.440 align:middle line:84%
So Miller and Weinberg is a
very similar paper to Bresnahan,

01:10:51.440 --> 01:10:57.860 align:middle line:84%
and it's a much more modern,
well-done, frontier quality

01:10:57.860 --> 01:11:00.230 align:middle line:90%
piece of applied work.

01:11:00.230 --> 01:11:04.370 align:middle line:84%
The applied question is
whether the rise in US beer

01:11:04.370 --> 01:11:08.210 align:middle line:84%
prices following the 2008 joint
venture of Miller and Coors

01:11:08.210 --> 01:11:11.450 align:middle line:84%
can be explained as a
consequence of just static price

01:11:11.450 --> 01:11:14.870 align:middle line:84%
competition or if we need
some degree of cooperation

01:11:14.870 --> 01:11:18.170 align:middle line:84%
or collusion among the firms
to understand what happens.

01:11:18.170 --> 01:11:20.360 align:middle line:84%
And so what it does
is it estimates

01:11:20.360 --> 01:11:25.940 align:middle line:84%
a model that assumes that the
firms were paying static--

01:11:25.940 --> 01:11:27.860 align:middle line:84%
matching with static
differentiated product

01:11:27.860 --> 01:11:33.480 align:middle line:84%
competition before the merger,
and then after the merger,

01:11:33.480 --> 01:11:37.920 align:middle line:84%
MillerCoors and Anheuser-Busch
are partially cooperative.

01:11:37.920 --> 01:11:41.250 align:middle line:84%
And then the partial cooperative
model, what it does is--

01:11:41.250 --> 01:11:43.270 align:middle line:84%
Bresnahan had three
or four models.

01:11:43.270 --> 01:11:45.040 align:middle line:90%
They have a continuum of models.

01:11:45.040 --> 01:11:47.400 align:middle line:84%
The continuum of
models is assume

01:11:47.400 --> 01:11:50.190 align:middle line:84%
it's as if each firm is
maximizing their own profit

01:11:50.190 --> 01:11:53.230 align:middle line:84%
plus kappa times the
profit of the other firms.

01:11:53.230 --> 01:11:56.010 align:middle line:84%
So kappa equals one
would be full collusion,

01:11:56.010 --> 01:11:59.370 align:middle line:84%
kappa equals zero would
be static competition,

01:11:59.370 --> 01:12:03.450 align:middle line:84%
and kappa in between is some
degree of partial collusion

01:12:03.450 --> 01:12:05.970 align:middle line:90%
between the firms.

01:12:05.970 --> 01:12:08.580 align:middle line:84%
And then what they note
is that in this model,

01:12:08.580 --> 01:12:10.920 align:middle line:84%
with this continuum
of models, you

01:12:10.920 --> 01:12:13.710 align:middle line:84%
can test whether kappa
equals zero to say,

01:12:13.710 --> 01:12:17.130 align:middle line:84%
does the static competition
model explain what's going on

01:12:17.130 --> 01:12:23.160 align:middle line:84%
or do I need to have some
degree of collusion starting

01:12:23.160 --> 01:12:26.430 align:middle line:84%
after the MillerCoors
merger to understand

01:12:26.430 --> 01:12:28.770 align:middle line:90%
why prices are what they are?

01:12:28.770 --> 01:12:31.650 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, I won't go
into the details here.

01:12:31.650 --> 01:12:36.660 align:middle line:84%
But background, I think
even graduate students drink

01:12:36.660 --> 01:12:40.440 align:middle line:84%
craft beer these days, but
despite the incredible rise

01:12:40.440 --> 01:12:44.550 align:middle line:84%
in craft beer, most Americans
still drink really cheap beer.

01:12:44.550 --> 01:12:49.073 align:middle line:84%
So most Americans drink Bud
Light, Coors Light, Miller Lite.

01:12:49.073 --> 01:12:50.490 align:middle line:84%
At least most sales
because people

01:12:50.490 --> 01:12:53.700 align:middle line:90%
buy Bud Light in 30 packs.

01:12:53.700 --> 01:12:58.170 align:middle line:84%
But there's been tremendous
consolidation of beer ownership

01:12:58.170 --> 01:13:00.700 align:middle line:90%
in the past 20 years globally.

01:13:00.700 --> 01:13:02.790 align:middle line:84%
So basically, in every
country around the world,

01:13:02.790 --> 01:13:05.430 align:middle line:84%
the same three companies
sell all the beer.

01:13:05.430 --> 01:13:08.497 align:middle line:84%
And so the same companies
that sell Anheuser-Busch

01:13:08.497 --> 01:13:10.080 align:middle line:84%
in the United States
are also the ones

01:13:10.080 --> 01:13:13.590 align:middle line:84%
that sell Beck's in Germany and
sell Stella Artois in England

01:13:13.590 --> 01:13:14.440 align:middle line:90%
and whatever.

01:13:14.440 --> 01:13:18.095 align:middle line:84%
But these companies have put
together portfolios of beers.

01:13:18.095 --> 01:13:21.100 align:middle line:90%


01:13:21.100 --> 01:13:25.120 align:middle line:84%
Prior to the 2008
MillerCoors joint venture,

01:13:25.120 --> 01:13:29.450 align:middle line:84%
Anheuser-Busch, InBev had a
market share of about 35%,

01:13:29.450 --> 01:13:32.750 align:middle line:84%
Miller had a market
share of 18%,

01:13:32.750 --> 01:13:35.330 align:middle line:90%
Coors had a market share of 11%.

01:13:35.330 --> 01:13:37.870 align:middle line:84%
And again, those are market
shares in the whole beer market.

01:13:37.870 --> 01:13:40.100 align:middle line:84%
If you take away
the premium beers,

01:13:40.100 --> 01:13:42.340 align:middle line:84%
there are many submarkets
in which those firms

01:13:42.340 --> 01:13:44.980 align:middle line:90%
have much higher shares.

01:13:44.980 --> 01:13:49.480 align:middle line:84%
And despite the fact that these
were the overall market shares,

01:13:49.480 --> 01:13:53.950 align:middle line:84%
Miller and Coors were, in 2008,
allowed to form a joint venture

01:13:53.950 --> 01:13:56.650 align:middle line:90%
and sell their beer together.

01:13:56.650 --> 01:14:00.868 align:middle line:84%
So MillerCoors argued that,
yes, it looks like the second

01:14:00.868 --> 01:14:02.410 align:middle line:84%
and third biggest
firms in the market

01:14:02.410 --> 01:14:06.550 align:middle line:84%
are merging together to create
a three firm competition to two

01:14:06.550 --> 01:14:09.970 align:middle line:84%
firm competition, but this is
going to be good for consumers

01:14:09.970 --> 01:14:12.822 align:middle line:84%
because there's going to be
enough cost savings that we're

01:14:12.822 --> 01:14:14.780 align:middle line:84%
going to have an incentive
to lower our prices.

01:14:14.780 --> 01:14:17.747 align:middle line:84%
Particularly, Coors was
mostly brewed in Colorado

01:14:17.747 --> 01:14:20.330 align:middle line:84%
and then had to be shipped all
across the United States, which

01:14:20.330 --> 01:14:21.630 align:middle line:90%
is expensive.

01:14:21.630 --> 01:14:23.930 align:middle line:84%
They're like, if only we
could produce all the Coors

01:14:23.930 --> 01:14:26.097 align:middle line:84%
beer in all those Miller
plants that are distributed

01:14:26.097 --> 01:14:28.160 align:middle line:84%
around the country
and then share trucks

01:14:28.160 --> 01:14:30.800 align:middle line:84%
shipping the Miller and Coors
to all the beer distributors,

01:14:30.800 --> 01:14:33.170 align:middle line:84%
then we'd save on
the trucking costs,

01:14:33.170 --> 01:14:34.590 align:middle line:90%
we'd save on the shipping costs.

01:14:34.590 --> 01:14:37.610 align:middle line:84%
Costs will go down so much that
consumers will be better off.

01:14:37.610 --> 01:14:42.350 align:middle line:84%
And the justice department
approved the merger.

01:14:42.350 --> 01:14:45.650 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, you know,
very beautiful graph

01:14:45.650 --> 01:14:47.990 align:middle line:84%
just giving the
basic thing of, did

01:14:47.990 --> 01:14:50.220 align:middle line:84%
what the firms tell the
justice department come true?

01:14:50.220 --> 01:14:51.595 align:middle line:84%
Here are the graphs
of the prices

01:14:51.595 --> 01:14:53.810 align:middle line:84%
for Miller Lite, Bud
Light, Coors Light

01:14:53.810 --> 01:14:55.770 align:middle line:84%
leading up to the
time of the merger.

01:14:55.770 --> 01:15:00.620 align:middle line:84%
And you can see beer
prices were trending down.

01:15:00.620 --> 01:15:04.130 align:middle line:84%
After the merger, there was this
temporary sale on Miller Lite.

01:15:04.130 --> 01:15:06.860 align:middle line:84%
But then by a couple
months after the merger,

01:15:06.860 --> 01:15:09.150 align:middle line:90%
prices went up there.

01:15:09.150 --> 01:15:13.170 align:middle line:84%
So there's just a very
clear departure from trend,

01:15:13.170 --> 01:15:15.510 align:middle line:84%
and the prices of Bud
Light, Miller Lite,

01:15:15.510 --> 01:15:18.545 align:middle line:84%
Coors Light jump after
the MillerCoors merger.

01:15:18.545 --> 01:15:21.435 align:middle line:90%


01:15:21.435 --> 01:15:24.490 align:middle line:84%
Their competitor's
prices didn't do so much.

01:15:24.490 --> 01:15:27.000 align:middle line:84%
So the two largest
brands not sold

01:15:27.000 --> 01:15:30.570 align:middle line:84%
by Anheuser-Busch,
InBev, or MillerCoors

01:15:30.570 --> 01:15:33.450 align:middle line:90%
are Heineken and Corona.

01:15:33.450 --> 01:15:37.930 align:middle line:84%
Heineken and Corona
prices, you don't notice--

01:15:37.930 --> 01:15:40.620 align:middle line:84%
maybe you look and see
there's a little jump up here,

01:15:40.620 --> 01:15:43.380 align:middle line:84%
but it seems like
they're mostly on trend.

01:15:43.380 --> 01:15:45.720 align:middle line:84%
So if we take that as a
differences in differences thing

01:15:45.720 --> 01:15:47.520 align:middle line:84%
to say there doesn't seem to
be a difference in the cost

01:15:47.520 --> 01:15:49.650 align:middle line:84%
of producing beer, if
there's no difference

01:15:49.650 --> 01:15:51.930 align:middle line:84%
in the cost of producing
beer, what you get

01:15:51.930 --> 01:15:55.410 align:middle line:84%
is that the 12 packs
of the flagship brands

01:15:55.410 --> 01:15:58.000 align:middle line:90%
went up by about 10%.

01:15:58.000 --> 01:16:00.370 align:middle line:84%
Overall, prices
went up by about 5%.

01:16:00.370 --> 01:16:02.790 align:middle line:90%
Basically it's as if they--

01:16:02.790 --> 01:16:05.070 align:middle line:84%
if a 12 pack of Bud
Light Costs $10,

01:16:05.070 --> 01:16:06.940 align:middle line:84%
it's like they
went from 10 to 11.

01:16:06.940 --> 01:16:09.292 align:middle line:90%
The 20 packs, it seems like--

01:16:09.292 --> 01:16:12.790 align:middle line:84%
the 30 packs, they seem like
they went from 18 to 19.

01:16:12.790 --> 01:16:15.720 align:middle line:84%
But it's basically like
prices go up by 5% on average,

01:16:15.720 --> 01:16:19.545 align:middle line:84%
but 10% on the 12 pack of
the standard flagship beers.

01:16:19.545 --> 01:16:22.540 align:middle line:90%


01:16:22.540 --> 01:16:25.090 align:middle line:90%
So why does this happen?

01:16:25.090 --> 01:16:29.480 align:middle line:84%
Again, there are three
things going on here.

01:16:29.480 --> 01:16:32.770 align:middle line:84%
There are-- costs are
presumably going down

01:16:32.770 --> 01:16:36.370 align:middle line:84%
because they're now brewing
Coors all over the country.

01:16:36.370 --> 01:16:39.190 align:middle line:84%
But MillerCoors has this
unilateral incentive

01:16:39.190 --> 01:16:41.650 align:middle line:84%
to raise its prices
because it knows

01:16:41.650 --> 01:16:43.915 align:middle line:84%
that if someone goes
in to buy Coors Light

01:16:43.915 --> 01:16:45.790 align:middle line:84%
and the price is now
higher than they expect,

01:16:45.790 --> 01:16:47.540 align:middle line:84%
they may switch to
Miller Lite, and that's

01:16:47.540 --> 01:16:49.360 align:middle line:90%
not a bad thing anymore.

01:16:49.360 --> 01:16:52.750 align:middle line:84%
And then there's this possible
collusion, third effect.

01:16:52.750 --> 01:16:54.710 align:middle line:90%
So anyway, what do they do?

01:16:54.710 --> 01:16:58.790 align:middle line:84%
It's random coefficients,
logit demand.

01:16:58.790 --> 01:17:00.790 align:middle line:84%
Tobias will tell you more
about what that means.

01:17:00.790 --> 01:17:06.130 align:middle line:84%
But basically, your utility
from buying a brand j of beer

01:17:06.130 --> 01:17:07.840 align:middle line:84%
if you live in
region r at time t

01:17:07.840 --> 01:17:10.690 align:middle line:84%
is characteristics
of the beers times

01:17:10.690 --> 01:17:13.570 align:middle line:84%
beta minus a
disutility from paying

01:17:13.570 --> 01:17:16.580 align:middle line:84%
for the beers plus a
bunch of fixed effect.

01:17:16.580 --> 01:17:19.840 align:middle line:84%
The brand fixed effect,
the time fixed effect,

01:17:19.840 --> 01:17:22.210 align:middle line:84%
an unobserved
quality of the beer,

01:17:22.210 --> 01:17:26.860 align:middle line:84%
and this idiosyncratic epsilon
like in the Hotelling model.

01:17:26.860 --> 01:17:32.170 align:middle line:84%
Some people like Coors relative
to Budweiser just taste wise.

01:17:32.170 --> 01:17:34.090 align:middle line:84%
What's different from
the standard logit model

01:17:34.090 --> 01:17:37.210 align:middle line:84%
is it's a random coefficients
where the different people have

01:17:37.210 --> 01:17:39.850 align:middle line:84%
different preferences
over the characteristics,

01:17:39.850 --> 01:17:42.010 align:middle line:84%
and some people are
more price sensitive

01:17:42.010 --> 01:17:44.120 align:middle line:84%
and some people are
less price sensitive.

01:17:44.120 --> 01:17:46.240 align:middle line:84%
So the random coefficients
mean that not everyone

01:17:46.240 --> 01:17:48.760 align:middle line:90%
has the same coefficients.

01:17:48.760 --> 01:17:51.310 align:middle line:84%
Some people like
hoppiness and some people

01:17:51.310 --> 01:17:52.370 align:middle line:90%
don't like hoppiness.

01:17:52.370 --> 01:17:54.260 align:middle line:84%
Some people dislike
paying money.

01:17:54.260 --> 01:17:56.890 align:middle line:84%
Other people don't care so much
about how much they're paying.

01:17:56.890 --> 01:17:57.850 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:17:57.850 --> 01:17:59.600 align:middle line:90%
They estimate this model.

01:17:59.600 --> 01:18:02.680 align:middle line:84%
They have some instruments
for price I will skip over,

01:18:02.680 --> 01:18:05.810 align:middle line:84%
but they find a bunch
of charts like this.

01:18:05.810 --> 01:18:09.570 align:middle line:84%
So this chart says these
are price elasticities that

01:18:09.570 --> 01:18:12.760 align:middle line:84%
says if you raise the
price of Budweiser by 1%,

01:18:12.760 --> 01:18:17.810 align:middle line:84%
sales of Budweiser go down by
4.4%, or sales of Bud Light.

01:18:17.810 --> 01:18:19.550 align:middle line:84%
But then people will
buy other things.

01:18:19.550 --> 01:18:24.220 align:middle line:84%
And so in particular, Budweiser
sales will go up by 0.3%,

01:18:24.220 --> 01:18:27.190 align:middle line:84%
Coors sales will go
up by over 0.316%,

01:18:27.190 --> 01:18:30.220 align:middle line:84%
Coors Light sales
will go up by 0.351%,

01:18:30.220 --> 01:18:33.040 align:middle line:84%
Corona sales will
go up by 2.279%.

01:18:33.040 --> 01:18:35.010 align:middle line:84%
We have this sort of
substitution matrix

01:18:35.010 --> 01:18:36.810 align:middle line:90%
they've estimated.

01:18:36.810 --> 01:18:39.700 align:middle line:84%
And then they say it's
a two-step estimation.

01:18:39.700 --> 01:18:42.790 align:middle line:84%
First, we estimate demand to
find all the elasticities.

01:18:42.790 --> 01:18:48.570 align:middle line:84%
Then we say what would happen
under the different models

01:18:48.570 --> 01:18:50.160 align:middle line:90%
of behavior.

01:18:50.160 --> 01:18:52.098 align:middle line:90%
And let me see.

01:18:52.098 --> 01:18:53.140 align:middle line:90%
Two things I want to say.

01:18:53.140 --> 01:18:58.260 align:middle line:84%
One thing they find is that they
estimate which kappa model best

01:18:58.260 --> 01:19:01.680 align:middle line:84%
fits the data, and they find
that the post-period kappas

01:19:01.680 --> 01:19:05.080 align:middle line:84%
of like 0.25 to 0.37
best fit the data.

01:19:05.080 --> 01:19:08.490 align:middle line:84%
So it's as if the firms
Budweiser and Miller are

01:19:08.490 --> 01:19:10.080 align:middle line:84%
putting weight on
each other's prices

01:19:10.080 --> 01:19:12.210 align:middle line:84%
and starting to
price higher than

01:19:12.210 --> 01:19:16.510 align:middle line:90%
seems optimal after the merger.

01:19:16.510 --> 01:19:20.980 align:middle line:90%
And here's the counterfactual.

01:19:20.980 --> 01:19:26.670 align:middle line:84%
So this is what beer prices
of-- so 12 packs were, like, $9

01:19:26.670 --> 01:19:28.350 align:middle line:90%
or whatever before the merger.

01:19:28.350 --> 01:19:31.020 align:middle line:84%
So we have this long-term
trend of declining prices

01:19:31.020 --> 01:19:33.150 align:middle line:90%
of 12 packs of cheap beer.

01:19:33.150 --> 01:19:35.520 align:middle line:84%
They think absent
the merger, it would

01:19:35.520 --> 01:19:40.500 align:middle line:84%
have continued along this path
with some rises and falls.

01:19:40.500 --> 01:19:42.390 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if
that's due to costs.

01:19:42.390 --> 01:19:44.350 align:middle line:84%
But then we have three
things happening.

01:19:44.350 --> 01:19:48.840 align:middle line:84%
The first is this unilateral
incentive of MillerCoors

01:19:48.840 --> 01:19:50.795 align:middle line:84%
to raise its prices
because it knows

01:19:50.795 --> 01:19:53.170 align:middle line:84%
that customers it loses when
it raises the price of Coors

01:19:53.170 --> 01:19:56.010 align:middle line:84%
go to Miller, and that's
no longer a bad thing.

01:19:56.010 --> 01:19:59.810 align:middle line:84%
That would cause prices
to go up for this much.

01:19:59.810 --> 01:20:03.390 align:middle line:84%
But then there were savings from
Coors being cheaper to produce,

01:20:03.390 --> 01:20:05.720 align:middle line:84%
and they say that the
Coors savings would

01:20:05.720 --> 01:20:07.280 align:middle line:90%
have been enough to offset--

01:20:07.280 --> 01:20:09.487 align:middle line:84%
that Coors was not
lying before the merger

01:20:09.487 --> 01:20:11.570 align:middle line:84%
when they said that our
costs are going to go down

01:20:11.570 --> 01:20:13.640 align:middle line:84%
by enough that it's going
to offset our incentive

01:20:13.640 --> 01:20:15.530 align:middle line:84%
to raise prices,
and they would say

01:20:15.530 --> 01:20:17.510 align:middle line:90%
the cost savings are this big.

01:20:17.510 --> 01:20:20.240 align:middle line:84%
So that would have
caused this to happen.

01:20:20.240 --> 01:20:23.840 align:middle line:84%
However, the third
effect is that it

01:20:23.840 --> 01:20:26.330 align:middle line:84%
looks like they're now
coordinating their pricing,

01:20:26.330 --> 01:20:28.250 align:middle line:84%
and that increase
in collusion is

01:20:28.250 --> 01:20:31.670 align:middle line:84%
enough to go from these
triangles to this line up here.

01:20:31.670 --> 01:20:33.710 align:middle line:84%
So they're arguing is
that there are three

01:20:33.710 --> 01:20:36.310 align:middle line:90%
roughly equally sized effect.

01:20:36.310 --> 01:20:40.060 align:middle line:84%
The MillerCoors
internalizing, it's

01:20:40.060 --> 01:20:43.360 align:middle line:84%
doing multi-product monopoly
pricing, the cost savings

01:20:43.360 --> 01:20:47.530 align:middle line:84%
on producing Coors, and
the Anheuser-Busch Miller

01:20:47.530 --> 01:20:49.510 align:middle line:90%
cooperative behavior.

01:20:49.510 --> 01:20:52.420 align:middle line:84%
All roughly equal sizes
but combining together

01:20:52.420 --> 01:20:54.195 align:middle line:84%
to cause this increase
in the 12 packs.

01:20:54.195 --> 01:20:57.600 align:middle line:90%


01:20:57.600 --> 01:21:01.290 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, I think, to appreciate--
the paper has some strengths

01:21:01.290 --> 01:21:02.560 align:middle line:90%
and weaknesses or whatever.

01:21:02.560 --> 01:21:06.240 align:middle line:84%
To appreciate those it's easier
after Tobias's Monday lecture,

01:21:06.240 --> 01:21:07.980 align:middle line:84%
so I figured I would
just skip those

01:21:07.980 --> 01:21:12.210 align:middle line:84%
and maybe you can go back and
reread the paper after Tobias's

01:21:12.210 --> 01:21:15.383 align:middle line:84%
talk next Monday
and think about what

01:21:15.383 --> 01:21:17.550 align:middle line:84%
do we think of the various
aspects of the estimation

01:21:17.550 --> 01:21:19.150 align:middle line:90%
strategy.

01:21:19.150 --> 01:21:24.430 align:middle line:84%
But it is a Bresnahan-like idea
of estimate multiple models,

01:21:24.430 --> 01:21:26.230 align:middle line:84%
see which ones fit
best, and that's

01:21:26.230 --> 01:21:30.030 align:middle line:84%
how I determine how
firms are behaving.

01:21:30.030 --> 01:21:30.530 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:21:30.530 --> 01:21:32.740 align:middle line:90%
Any questions?

01:21:32.740 --> 01:21:34.610 align:middle line:90%
OK, then we are done.

01:21:34.610 --> 01:21:37.310 align:middle line:90%
I guess I get a week off.

01:21:37.310 --> 01:21:40.720 align:middle line:90%
Tobias is going to be here.

01:21:40.720 --> 01:21:43.510 align:middle line:84%
Gandhi and Nevo is published
in whatever handbook

01:21:43.510 --> 01:21:45.710 align:middle line:84%
of IO that's expensive
and hard to find.

01:21:45.710 --> 01:21:47.830 align:middle line:84%
But if you look at the
same exact document

01:21:47.830 --> 01:21:52.840 align:middle line:84%
as NBER working paper 29257,
so if you look at that version,

01:21:52.840 --> 01:21:55.050 align:middle line:90%
that's easy to find.

01:21:55.050 --> 01:22:07.000 align:middle line:90%